.TH KOSH 1 "2026-08-18" "kosh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kosh \- a fast and debuggable POSIX- and Bash-compatible command shell,
formatter, and language server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kosh
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ script
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-s
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-lint
.RI [ \-c
.IR command ...]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-format
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-format
.B \-\-apply
.I file ...
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-lint
.RI [ \-\-format ]
.B \-\-apply
.I file ...
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-as\-language\-server
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kosh
is the Koshka shell executable. It is an interpreter, an interactive shell, a
formatter, a language server, and a diagnostic tool. It accepts full Bash and
POSIX shell syntax with Bash-compatible extensions.
.PP
Koshka includes about 300 built-in ShellCheck diagnostics. Script execution is
about five times faster than Bash, and analysis is about 100 times faster than
ShellCheck.
.PP
Koshka supports Linux, macOS, and Windows with equivalent behavior on all
three.
.PP
The grammar is the same on every operating system. An unquoted backslash is an
escape on Windows. A native Windows path uses a quoted backslash, an escaped
backslash, or a forward slash. After quote removal, Windows path
operations accept both slash forms and recognize drive and UNC roots. POSIX
path operations accept only a forward slash.
.SH INVOCATION
The input source is selected in this order. The
.B \-s
option selects standard input. One or more
.B \-c
options select their command strings in occurrence order. A script operand
selects that file. The
.B \-i
option or a terminal on standard input selects the interactive editor when no
earlier source applies. Standard input is used otherwise.
.PP
Without
.BR \-s ,
.B \-\-lint
analyzes each command string in occurrence order, followed by every file
operand. Lint operation is never interactive, and the shell exits after the
last selected input.
Under
.BR \-s ,
standard input is analyzed and a file operand becomes a positional parameter.
With no command string and no file operand, standard input is analyzed.
.PP
The
.B \-\-format
option takes standard input when no file is named. One named file is formatted
to standard output. The
.B \-\-apply
option writes the result to one or more named files. Under
.BR \-\-lint ,
safe diagnostic fixes are written. When
.B \-\-lint
and
.B \-\-format
are combined, the lint fixes are written first and the result is then
formatted.
.B \-\-lint \-\-format
without
.B \-\-apply
writes the fixed and formatted source to standard output.
.PP
The
.B \-\-format
option separates a declaration block from the following command. It also
separates a completed fi, done, esac, subshell, or brace group from the next
independent command. Lines are kept within 78 columns. A word that cannot be
broken to fit is left whole, and a warning identifies its file, line, and
column.
.PP
When both
.B \-s
and
.B \-i
are present outside lint operation, a terminal on standard input selects
interactive operation. A non-terminal standard input is read as input. The
.B \-s
option takes precedence over command strings and file operands. Command strings
take precedence over
.BR \-i .
A script operand takes precedence over
.BR \-i .
.PP
The invocation basename selects an initial mood. The names
.B sh
and
.B dash
select the
.B sh
mood. The names
.B bash
and
.B rbash
select the
.B bash
mood. The name
.B rbash
also requests restricted mode. A dash at the start of argument zero or the
.B \-l
option selects login operation.
.PP
The
.B KOSH_FLAGS
environment variable supplies options before the command line is parsed. A
command-line option overrides the corresponding value from
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
The
.B \-c
option is ignored in
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
The
.BR \-\-format ,
.BR \-\-apply ,
and
.B \-\-as\-language\-server
options are also ignored there.
.PP
The shell enters rescue mode when argument zero begins with a dash, its options
cannot be parsed, and standard input is a terminal. Rescue mode clears the
options and skips startup files. The
.B \-l
option alone does not enable this recovery. Any other invalid invocation exits
with status 2.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS General options
.TP
.B \-\-version
The version and notices are displayed. The shell then exits.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-short\-version
The short version is displayed. The shell then exits.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Help is displayed. The shell then exits.
.SS POSIX options
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
The shell runs interactively.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stdin
Commands are read from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-c " " \fICOMMAND\fR ", " \-\-command =\fICOMMAND\fR
COMMAND is read and run. The option may be repeated, and the command strings
run in the order they are given.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-error\-exit
The shell exits after an unguarded failing command. A function called from a
non-final operand of
.B &&
or
.BR || ,
or from a negation, stays guarded through its whole body.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-no\-glob
Pathname expansion is disabled.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-one\-command
One command is read and run. The shell then exits.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Each input line is printed as it is read.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-xtrace
Each command is printed after expansion.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-export\-all
Each later assignment is marked for export.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-no\-clobber
An existing regular file is not overwritten by a plain output redirection; a
device or FIFO still takes the output.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-exec
Commands are parsed and analyzed without being run. The analysis stage runs in
every mood under this option, and a compatibility mood still reports only the
tiers selected by
.BR \-W .
A runtime
.B set \-n
also suppresses later commands in the current command list.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-no\-unset
An unset variable read is an error.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
The shell acts as a login shell.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-m
The options are accepted without effect for compatibility.
.TP
.BR \-o " " \fINAME\fR ", " +o " " \fINAME\fR
The option NAME is enabled with
.B set \-o
.I NAME
and disabled with
.BR +o
.I NAME
, as
.B set \-o
.I NAME
and
.B set +o
.I NAME
do, respectively. A name with a letter, such as errexit, acts as that letter,
and the later setting wins.
.B \-o posix
acts as
.BR \-\-posix .
An unknown name exits with status 2.
.TP
.BR +a ", " +C ", " +e ", " +f ", " +n ", " +p ", " +t ", " +u ", " +v ", " +x
The letter's option is turned off, as with
.BR "set +e" .
Letters may be combined, as in
.BR +eu ,
and
.B \-euo pipefail
sets pipefail with
.BR \-o .
.PP
An option named on the command line, on or off, keeps that value over the
settings files and KOSHCONF, so
.B kosh +e script
runs the script without errexit even when a settings file turns it on.
.TP
.B \-\-
Option parsing ends. The next operand is treated as the script name.
.SS Bash options
.TP
.BR \-\-init\-file " " \fIFILE\fR ", " \-\-rcfile " " \fIFILE\fR
FILE selects the interactive rc file. The last occurrence of either name is
used.
A leading tilde is expanded. The file replaces ~/.bashrc in an interactive
non-login Bash mood. The system Bash rc is still read first. The
.B kosh
mood sources no rc file and ignores this option.
.TP
.B \-\-norc
The interactive non-login Bash rc and a custom rc file are skipped. Login
profiles and noninteractive BASH_ENV processing are unchanged. The stock Bash
completion script is not loaded.
.TP
.BR \-O " " \fINAME\fR ", " +O " " \fINAME\fR
The shopt option NAME is turned on, or off with
.BR +O ,
as
.B shopt \-s
.I NAME
and
.B shopt \-u
.I NAME
do, and keeps that value over the settings files. A read-only name such as
login_shell is accepted without effect. An unknown name exits with status 2.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-restricted
The shell becomes restricted after the startup files finish.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-privileged
Privileged mode is enabled. BASH_ENV is not sourced. KOSHCONF from the
environment is ignored and removed. The settings files are still read. When
the real and effective ids differ, every startup file and settings file is
skipped. Elevated ids are retained
only when this option is present. The state is exposed through
.BR $\- ,
.BR SHELLOPTS ,
and
.BR "set \-o privileged" .
.TP
.B \-\-posix
The
.B bash-posix
mood is selected.
.SS Compatibility options
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-mood =\fINAME\fR
NAME selects
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash ,
.BR sh ,
or
.BR bash-posix .
The name
.B default
is accepted for kosh, and
.B posix
and
.B dash
are accepted for sh.
When no mood is chosen and the shell runs a script file operand, the first line
of the script selects the mood. A bash shebang selects
.BR bash ,
an sh or dash shebang selects
.BR sh ,
and a kosh shebang selects
.BR kosh .
The interpreter may be selected through
.BR env ,
as in
.B "#!/usr/bin/env bash"
or
.BR "#!/usr/bin/env \-S bash \-e" .
Any other interpreter, or no shebang, leaves the
.B kosh
mood. The mood is chosen before startup files are read. An explicit
.BR \-\-mood ,
.BR \-\-dumb ,
.BR \-\-posix ,
or a shell invocation name such as sh or bash takes precedence over the shebang.
The shebang is not read for
.BR \-c ,
standard input, or
.BR \-\-lint .
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-init\-moods =\fILIST\fR
Startup files for each listed mood are sourced in order. LIST accepts
comma-separated names. The option may be repeated. When the option is omitted,
an interactive or login shell uses init_moods from the configuration,
and otherwise the selected runtime mood.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-enable\-mimicry
A script run as a command with a recognized shell shebang runs under the
corresponding mood. A script operand always does, with or without this
option. A recognized shell invocation name, an explicit
.BR \-\-mood ,
.BR \-\-dumb ,
or
.B \-\-posix
takes precedence over the shebang. The current diagnostic setting is retained.
When
.B set
changes the option, launched shells inherit it through
.BR KOSH_ANALYSIS .
.TP
.B \-\-dumb
The options
.BR "\-\-mood sh" ,
.BR \-\-no\-completion ,
.BR "\-\-tab\-selector plain" ,
and
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
are applied. Color is disabled. An explicit
.B \-\-tab\-selector
overrides the plain selector, wherever it appears.
.SS Auxiliary options
.TP
.B \-\-lint
The option behaves like
.B \-n
with every diagnostic tier enabled at its normal severity. Diagnostic
suppression from
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
and
.B \-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics
is overridden. Every startup file is skipped. Aliases, functions, and the search
path from the invoking session do not affect the report.
.PP
The kind of a named input is detected from its path, extension, and content.
Shell commands are extracted from GitHub Actions, Gitea Actions, Forgejo
Actions, GitLab CI, Ansible, Dockerfile and Containerfile, Compose, Markdown,
Makefile, Google Cloud Build, CircleCI, Azure Pipelines, Bitbucket Pipelines,
Buildkite, Travis CI, Kubernetes, package.json scripts, Drone, Woodpecker,
Taskfile, Justfile, RPM spec files, VSCode tasks, and Dev Container files. Only
the extracted sh, bash, or kosh source is parsed and diagnosed. Jenkinsfile,
Terraform, Nix, and an unrecognized
YAML, JSON, or Markdown host are not treated as whole shell files.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-apply ,
safe structured fixes are written to each named file. The updated source is
analyzed again, and only the remaining diagnostics are reported. The written
fixes are then summarized, with one line for each diagnostic slug and its
ShellCheck code, counted as warnings or errors. A warning-only result exits
successfully. A remaining error or an input or write failure exits with status
1.
.TP
.B \-\-format
Shell source is formatted without being run. Structural keywords and compound
braces are placed on separate lines. Each nested level uses two spaces. Direct
.B test
commands become bracket commands unless an earlier alias or function shadows
the name. Code is wrapped at 80 columns when a safe token boundary exists.
Standalone comments follow the surrounding code indentation and are not
wrapped. Inline comments remain inline. Here-document bodies and terminators
retain their layout, and the shell source around them is still formatted.
Output uses LF line endings and one final newline for a nonempty script.
Formatted source uses syntax colors when standard output is a terminal.
With no file or a lone
.B \-
operand, source is read from standard input. One named file is formatted to
standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Named files are updated with safe lint fixes under
.BR \-\-lint ,
or with formatted source under
.BR \-\-format .
The two modes may be combined. Before a changed file is replaced atomically, its
contents and identity are checked again. File modes and symbolic links are
retained. Other hard links continue to refer to the previous inode. Standard
input,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-c ,
and a lone
.B \-
operand are rejected. This option is ignored in
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
.TP
.B \-\-as\-language\-server
The language server protocol is run over standard input and standard output.
The editor starts this mode and associates it with
.BR *.sh ,
.BR *.dash ,
.BR *.bash ,
and
.BR *.kosh .
The server provides diagnostics, quick fixes, completion, navigation, command
information, semantic tokens, a document outline, and rename. A fix-all action
applies every non-conflicting safe fix in the open document. At command
position, completion includes shell keywords, builtins, bundled utilities,
functions and aliases defined in the document, and commands from the active
PATH.
The client receives keyword items as keywords and all other items as functions.
Each command item contains the completed name. The client requests its help text
when it resolves the item. Help text is fetched only for the highlighted item.
.PP
The server recognizes embedded shell regions in the host formats described
under
.BR \-\-lint .
Language features operate only inside those regions. The editor's host language
service handles the host syntax.
The outline is a tree of
the function definitions and the variable assignments in the document. An
assignment inside a function body is a child of that function, and one name
assigned again in the same body is one outline row. A name bound by
.BR local ,
.BR declare ,
.BR typeset ,
.BR export ,
.BR readonly ,
.BR for ,
.BR select ,
.BR read ,
.BR mapfile ,
.BR readarray ,
.BR getopts ,
.BR printf " " \-v ,
or an arithmetic assignment is a variable assignment too. Hover over a variable
reports the nearest preceding assignment and its folded value. If the assignment
has no folded value, hover describes its source, such as a loop word, a field
read from input, or an arithmetic expression. Earlier assignments in the same
document are listed below it. An assignment that does not run on every path is
marked. A shell-defined variable uses the description from the builtin variable
catalog. Hover over a function shows its definition body as printed by
.BR "declare \-f" .
Hover over a command the document does not define reports the external
help text. A builtin is checked first, followed by a program on the active PATH,
then a bundled utility when PATH has no matching program. Markdown is used when
the client advertises support for it.
Rename updates variables, functions, and aliases throughout the open document. A
read written as
.B $name
or
.B ${name}
keeps its sigils and braces. Only the name bytes are replaced. A command can be
renamed only when the document defines it as a function or alias. Builtins,
bundled utilities, and programs on the active PATH cannot be renamed. A new
variable name may contain letters, digits, and underscores, but may not begin
with a digit. A new command name may not contain shell metacharacters. A
recognized shebang selects the mood first. If there is no recognized shebang,
the client language identifier selects it. If the client sends no recognized
identifier, a recognized document extension selects it. An unrecognized document
uses the
.B kosh
mood. This option does not accept file operands,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-\-lint ,
.BR \-\-format ,
or
.BR \-\-apply .
It is ignored in
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
.SS Koshka options
.TP
.BR \-Q ", " \-\-no\-init\-files
No startup file is read. PATH is set to /usr/bin:/bin.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
No settings file is read, and KOSHCONF from the environment is ignored and
removed. PATH and the shell startup files are left alone, so a script can run
with the default settings without the minimal PATH of
.BR \-Q .
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-WW ", " \-WWW
The analysis stage has strict, lenient, and annoying tiers. In the default
mood, strict and lenient diagnostics are errors, while annoying diagnostics are
warnings. The
.B \-W
form retains those severities,
.B \-WW
demotes lenient errors to warnings, and
.B \-WWW
also demotes strict errors. In other moods,
.B \-W
enables strict diagnostics as warnings,
.B \-WW
also enables lenient diagnostics, and
.B \-WWW
also enables annoying diagnostics. An explicit
.B set \-M
or
.B koshconf set mood
change clears the active level. A level changed with
.B set
is inherited by launched shells through
.BR KOSH_ANALYSIS ,
and an explicit level on the command line replaces it. In the Bash and POSIX
moods no analysis runs for the script or the files it sources until a level is
set, and an inherited
.BI warnings= N
counts as set. The
.B mimicry
word alone sets no level. Runtime diagnostics do not depend on the level.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-diagnostics
The available analysis checks and their metadata are listed. The shell then
exits.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
The analysis stage is skipped, and no warning or pre-run diagnostic is reported.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics
The annoying tier is suppressed. Strict and lenient analysis is unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-init\-diagnostics
Diagnostics and warnings are suppressed while startup files are sourced. The
previous settings are restored before interactive input begins.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-traces
Source backtraces are suppressed. The primary message, source caret, note, and
detail location remain visible when available. This turns
optimizer.show_source_traces off.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-no\-completion
Tab completion, ghost text, and syntax coloring are disabled. This turns
completion.on_tab off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-syntax\-highlighting
Syntax coloring and ghost text are disabled. Tab completion remains enabled.
This turns editor.highlight_syntax_and_show_ghost_text off.
.TP
.BR \-\-tab\-selector " " \fIMODE\fR
MODE selects how an explicit Tab presents several candidates and how Ctrl\-R
presents the history. Valid modes are
.BR interactive ,
.BR external ,
and
.BR plain .
The default is
.BR interactive .
The behavior of each mode is described under
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-koshkit
Bundled utility names resolve directly as commands. The option is equivalent to
.BR "koshconf set interpreter.resolve_koshkit_applets_as_commands on" .
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-extended\-arithmetic
Arbitrary-precision integers and finite decimal values are enabled. The option
is equivalent to
.BR "koshconf set interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers on" .
The
.B kosh
mood always holds this option on.
.SS Debug options
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-show\-ast
Syntax trees are printed before execution and during formatting or linting.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-optimizer\-diagnostics
Analysis prepass decisions are reported. Each folded or eliminated node produces
a lenient analysis diagnostic. The final line reports the number of eliminated
statements. A compatibility mood reports these diagnostics only under
.BR \-WW .
.TP
.B \-\-show\-exit\-code
A located diagnostic is printed when a command returns a non-zero status. A
warning is printed when execution continues. An error is printed before
errexit exits. A pipeline reports its effective status after pipefail is
applied. The
.B "koshconf set debug.report_nonzero_exit_codes on"
form turns the diagnostic on while the shell runs.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-show\-all\-exit\-codes
Every command status is reported, including a successful zero. A zero status is
always a warning. A non-zero status keeps the wording and the severity of
.BR \-\-show\-exit\-code .
The
.BR "set \-N" " and " "set +N"
forms turn the diagnostic on and off while the shell runs.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-show\-lexed\-words
The escape bitmap is printed after each parsed command.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-stats
Command, expansion, node, and arena statistics are printed after each command.
The report also counts the builtins, functions, and external commands this
shell ran, the processes it forked and executed, the bytes and system calls it
read and wrote, and its resource usage: user and system time, peak resident
set, context switches, and page faults. A figure the platform cannot report is
omitted. Windows counts each created process as one fork and one exec.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-memory
Arena and heap usage is printed at exit.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " \-\-debug\-logging =\fILEVEL\fR
Internal logging is enabled at
.BR info ,
.BR debug ,
or
.BR all .
An unknown level is a usage error. This option is available only in a debug
build.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-logging\-file =\fIFILE\fR
Debug logging is appended to FILE. A missing file is created. Standard error is
used when the option is omitted or the file cannot be opened. This option is
available only in a debug build.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-complete\-at =\fILINE\fR
Completion candidates for LINE are printed. The shell then exits. This option
is available only in a debug build.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-highlight\-at =\fILINE\fR
Highlight spans for LINE are printed. The shell then exits. This option is
available only in a debug build.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-ghost\-at =\fILINE\fR
The ghost completion result and operation counts for LINE are printed. The
shell then exits. This option is available only in a debug build.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-brackets\-at =\fILINE\fR
The bracket pair matched at each caret offset of LINE is printed. The shell
then exits. This option is available only in a debug build.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-arena\-lifetimes
Arena lifetime identities are checked across every release and reset. This
option is available only in a debug build.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-toiletline\-allocation
Allocation failure handling in the line editor is checked. This option is
available only in a debug build.
.TP
.BR \-\-ftrace ", " \-\-strace
Function or system-call tracing is enabled. These options are available only in
a Cosmopolitan build.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I script
When neither
.B \-s
nor
.B \-c
selects another source, the first operand specifies the script file. A lone
.B \-
operand selects standard input. A directory operand fails with status 126. A
file that cannot be opened fails with status 127.
.TP
.I argument
For a script invocation, the script pathname becomes $0. Arguments after the
script become positional parameters. For a
.B \-c
invocation, the first operand after COMMAND becomes $0. Later operands become
positional parameters. For
.B \-s
and interactive operation, $0 remains the invocation name and every operand
becomes a positional parameter.
.PP
Without
.BR \-s ,
.B \-\-lint
treats every operand as an input file and no operand becomes a positional
parameter. Missing, unreadable, and directory inputs are reported before the
remaining inputs are analyzed.
.SH MOODS
The
.B kosh
mood enables strict checks, pipefail, failglob, nounset, extended arithmetic,
and analysis by default. The
.B bash
mood accepts Bash extensions with analysis disabled by default. The
.B sh
mood follows the supported dash behavior. The
.B bash-posix
mood uses Bash syntax with the POSIX option enabled and sources the Bash startup
files.
.PP
The
.B kosh
mood holds nounset, pipefail, and failglob on, extended arithmetic on, and
nullglob off.
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
and
.B koshconf
report an error that identifies the mood and suggests
.B koshconf set mood bash
when asked for another value, and accept the value the mood already holds, so
.B set \-euo pipefail
still runs. A function defined in another mood cannot relax them for a caller
in the
.B kosh
mood. Every other option that changes evaluation remains toggleable in the
.B kosh
mood. Errexit aborts when commands fail, including failures that scripts test.
Noclobber and noglob prevent ordinary file and pattern use. Xtrace, verbose,
allexport, and the other Bash options select behavior. In the
.BR bash ,
.BR bash-posix ,
and
.B sh
moods every Bash option is toggleable through
.B set
and
.BR shopt .
An explicit
.BR "set \-u" ,
.BR "set \-o pipefail" ,
.BR "shopt \-s failglob" ,
or
.B koshconf set interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers
in such a mood survives a later change between those moods. The command
.B set \-o posix
selects
.BR bash-posix .
The command
.B set +o posix
selects
.B bash
only from the
.B bash-posix
or
.B sh
mood.
.PP
An error that ends a non-interactive shell in every mood, such as an unset
parameter read under
.B set \-u
or ${name?word} with name unset, passes through an eval, a sourced file, and
the other commands that run text in the current shell, as it does in Bash and
dash. A subshell and a command substitution still hold it. A trap action and a
startup file hold it too, and report the error.
.PP
On Windows, the default mood leaves backslashes literal in
.BR echo .
The
.B \-e
option enables escape decoding. The
.B sh
mood retains dash-style default escape handling.
.PP
On Windows, a path of
.B /dev/null
opens the null device, and a path of
.B /tmp
or a path under it maps to the same path under the temporary directory held
in
.BR TEMP ,
so a script that writes under
.B /tmp
runs unchanged. A longer name such as
.B /tmpx
is not mapped. The options
.B compatibility.dev_null
and
.BR compatibility.slash_tmp ,
both on by default, govern the two mappings, and turning one off leaves its
path to the current drive. Other platforms keep both paths unchanged.
.SH OPTION REGISTRY
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR koshconf ,
and the
.B \-o
test read and write one registry. Each option has a numeric id that never
changes meaning, a koshconf name, its Bash spelling when it is a Bash option,
its letter when it has one, a type, and a class. A boolean is on or off and is
named as a statement that is true when it is on. An enumeration is written by
name and encoded by position. A string is written as it is. An interactive
option affects only the interactive editor, the history, or how diagnostics
are presented, so a script runs the same under every value. Every other option
is semantic: it can change what a script does or what it writes to its output,
as the analysis stage, command resolution, mimicry, and the debug reports do.
.PP
A koshconf name starts with its topic. Related settings sort and search
together. Every globbing option contains glob. The topics are
.BR mood
and
.BR init_moods ,
which select the mood,
.BR interpreter ,
for the behavior of the shell itself,
.BR compatibility ,
for the Windows mapping of POSIX paths,
.BR editor ,
for the line editor with its subtopics
.BR editor.history
and
.BR editor.hints ,
.BR completion ,
.BR debug ,
and
.BR optimizer ,
for the analysis stage and its reports.
.B koshconf list
shows the current value of each.
.PP
The canonical name of an option inherited from POSIX
.B set \-o
carries a posix segment before its last part, and one inherited from any other
Bash
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
option carries a bash segment there, as in
interpreter.posix.exit_on_command_failure for errexit and
interpreter.bash.glob_extended_patterns for extglob. A name without either
segment belongs to kosh alone.
.PP
Every option that has a Bash spelling is also accepted under an alias. The
alias is legacy.posix.NAME when POSIX
.B set \-o
accepts NAME. Otherwise it is legacy.bash.NAME when
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
accepts NAME. Examples include legacy.bash.extglob, legacy.bash.failglob,
legacy.bash.braceexpand, and legacy.bash.posix. An alias resolves to the same
option entry as the canonical name, with the same id and the same storage, so
setting either spelling changes the value shown by the other spelling,
.BR "set \-o" ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR $\- ,
and SHELLOPTS. It is a second spelling resolved during lookup. A
kosh.conf line, koshconf set, and koshconf get accept the alias, as does every
option-name operand. A kosh.conf line that sets an option twice through both
spellings follows the duplicate rule of the file.
.B koshconf create
and
.BR "koshconf list" ,
error messages, and KOSHCONF use only the canonical name, except that
.B koshconf list \-\-all
adds each alias on a line of its own.
.PP
A configuration file may set every option except the ones fixed by how the
shell started and the ones that depend on the session. interpreter.bash.login_shell and
interpreter.bash.restricted_shell are read-only. interpreter.bash.privileged_mode,
interpreter.posix.parse_without_executing, and interpreter.bash.exit_after_one_command come from the
command line. interpreter.posix.job_control, editor.history.bash.records_commands,
editor.history.bash.bang_expansion, and interpreter.bash.posixly_correct follow the session and
the mood. editor.history.file_path may appear in a file, but
.B KOSHCONF
leaves it out.
.B KOSHCONF
carries only the mood and the interactive options.
.PP
editor.base_mode is one option behind the Bash names emacs and vi. Its
value is vi while
.B set \-o vi
is on and emacs otherwise, since the line editor uses emacs keys whenever vi is
off.
.BR "set \-o emacs" ,
.BR "set \-o vi" ,
their
.B +o
forms, the
.B \-o
test, and SHELLOPTS keep the Bash behavior, including the state where neither
is on, which a non-interactive shell starts in. Writing vi turns vi on and emacs
off; writing emacs turns vi off and emacs on when vi was on. The id 88 is
retired.
.PP
The Bash options hashall and nolog have no koshconf name, since neither changes
what Koshka does: Koshka caches command paths whether or not hashall is on, and
Bash itself ignores nolog.
.B set
and
.B \-o
still accept both for Bash scripts that query them. The ids 68 and 86 are
retired.
.PP
