.PP
The values are mood, one of kosh, sh, bash, and bash-posix;
completion.menu_style, one of interactive, external, and plain;
optimizer.warning_level, one of 0, 1, 2, and 3; and editor.base_mode,
one of emacs and vi. editor.history.file_path and editor.history.max_entries are
the
KOSH_HISTORY_FILE and KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE variables. init_moods is a
comma-separated list of the moods that
.B \-L
accepts, and is empty by default. It is read once at startup, applies only to an
interactive or login shell, and yields to
.BR \-L .
A restricted shell ignores it in the configuration files. Changing it in a
running shell sources nothing; use
.B set \-L
for that. completion.on_tab, editor.highlight_syntax_and_show_ghost_text,
and optimizer.show_source_traces are the settings behind
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-\-no\-syntax\-highlighting ,
and
.BR \-\-no\-traces .
The editor applies the first two at the next prompt. Syntax coloring and ghost
text need completion.on_tab as well. interactive-comments is both
a
.B set \-o
and a
.B shopt
name.
.SS Migration
.B set \-\-mood
is
.B set \-M
or
.BR "koshconf set mood" ,
.B set \-\-init\-moods
is
.BR "set \-L" ,
and
.B set \-\-tab\-selector
is
.BR "koshconf set completion.menu_style" .
Each former Koshka
.B set \-o
name is a koshconf option:
.B set \-o koshkit
is
.BR "koshconf set interpreter.resolve_koshkit_applets_as_commands on" ,
and interactive-hints, interactive-diagnostics, auto-pair, transient-prompt,
extended-keys, history-prefix-search, space-after-completion,
annoying-diagnostics, no-diagnostics, extended-arithmetic, mimicry, and the
show options are editor.hints.show_command_synopsis,
editor.hints.show_live_diagnostics,
editor.auto_close_brackets_and_quotes, editor.transient_prompt_after_submit,
editor.request_extended_key_reports,
editor.history.arrow_keys_search_by_typed_prefix,
completion.add_space_after_completed_word,
optimizer.show_annoying_tier,
optimizer.analyze_before_running with the opposite value,
interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers, interpreter.mimic_shebang,
and the debug options. failglob is
.B shopt \-s failglob
only. The hyphenated spellings such as no-unset are gone. ~/.koshrc is no longer
read; see
.BR kosh (5).
.SH STARTUP FILES
Before any shell startup file, interactive and noninteractive shells apply the
configuration files /etc/kosh.conf, or %ProgramData%\kosh\kosh.conf on
Windows, and then $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf, or
~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset, empty, or relative.
The system file is skipped with a warning unless only the system
administrator can change it.
They are not shell code;
.BR kosh (5)
describes their format. A
.B KOSHCONF
variable in the environment is applied after them and removed from the
environment. A later setting replaces an earlier one. A mood selected by the
configuration selects the startup files below unless the command line or the
invocation name selects a mood, and a script shebang still selects the mood of
that script. Any option specified on the command line, and any diagnostic
setting inherited through KOSH_ANALYSIS, keeps that value. A malformed line is
reported
with its file and line and skipped.
.PP
The
.B kosh
mood sources no shell startup file, so ~/.koshrc and /etc/koshrc are not read
and
.B \-\-rcfile
has no effect in it. Shell code meant to run at startup belongs in ~/.bashrc
with the
.B bash
mood selected. A
.B kosh
mood session started with
.B \-L bash
sources the bash startup files in the
.B bash
mood and then returns to the
.B kosh
mood. The init_moods option in a configuration file does the same for
every interactive or login shell started without
.BR \-L ;
see
.BR kosh (5).
Every mood sets KOSH and never exports it, so a real bash sees it unset. A line
guarded with
.B test \-n \(dq$KOSH\(dq
runs only in Koshka, and a line guarded with
.B test \-z \(dq$KOSH\(dq
runs only in another shell.
.PP
Each startup file is parsed with the grammar of the mood whose files are being
sourced. A startup file that invokes
.B "set \-L"
does not reenter a mood whose files are already being sourced.
.PP
A login shell in the
.B sh
mood sources /etc/profile and ~/.profile in that order.
.PP
An interactive shell in the
.B sh
mood sources the file named by ENV when privileged mode is disabled. The ENV
value is used as a pathname without PATH lookup.
.PP
A login shell in the
.B bash
or
.B bash-posix
mood sources /etc/profile. It then sources the first readable file among
~/.bash_profile, ~/.bash_login, and ~/.profile.
.PP
An interactive non-login shell in the
.B bash
or
.B bash-posix
mood sources the first readable file among /etc/bash/bashrc and
/etc/bash.bashrc. It then sources the custom rc file or ~/.bashrc. The file
/usr/share/bash-completion/bash_completion is sourced after the rc file. It is
skipped when completion is disabled, when a default completion specification
exists, and when BASH_COMPLETION_VERSINFO is already set.
.PP
A noninteractive shell in the
.B bash
mood sources BASH_ENV once. Parameter expansion, command substitution,
arithmetic expansion, and leading-tilde expansion are applied to the value.
PATH is not searched. A shell in the
.B bash-posix
mood does not source BASH_ENV.
.PP
A clean, rescue, lint, format, or language server shell skips every startup
file and every configuration file, and so does
.BR \-Q .
A shell whose real and effective identities differ skips them too. A fresh
evaluator inherits its parent's settings. The
privileged option suppresses ENV and BASH_ENV processing. Restricted mode is
visible while startup files run. Its restrictions become active after startup
finishes.
.PP
Settings for pipefail and failglob that come from the mood remain disabled while
startup files run. An explicit command-line nounset setting remains active. The
selected mood's strictness takes effect after startup finishes.
.PP
Missing optional files, custom rc files, and BASH_ENV files are skipped. An
existing custom rc or BASH_ENV file that cannot be read produces a diagnostic
and is skipped. An unreadable ENV file is skipped without that diagnostic. See
.BR kosh (5)
for the startup file format.
.SH RESTRICTED SHELL
A restricted shell rejects directory changes and changes to SHELL, PATH, ENV,
and BASH_ENV. A directory separator is rejected in a command name, a source
operand, and a
.B hash \-p
path, and every file operand of
.B history
is rejected. The history files keep the paths derived from HOME at startup, so a
later HOME assignment does not move them.
.PP
The redirections >, >|, <>, >>, &>, &>>, and >& are rejected. Numeric
descriptor duplication remains available. The
.B exec
builtin,
.BR "command \-p" ,
and
.B enable \-f
or
.B enable \-d
are rejected. The
.B koshkit
builtin is rejected in every form, and a command name never falls back to a
bundled utility, so a restricted shell runs only the builtins and the programs
its PATH provides.
.BR type ,
.BR "command \-v" ,
and completion do not offer the bundled utilities. Restricted mode cannot be
disabled with
.BR "set +r" .
Changing the mood with
.B set \-M
and mimicry with
.B set \-I
or
.B set +I
is rejected.
.B set \-o posix
remains available, as in bash.
Changes to the restricted_shell shopt value are accepted without effect.
.PP
The
.B koshconf
forms
.BR create ,
.BR set ,
and
.B load
are rejected, so a restricted shell neither changes a setting nor writes a
configuration file;
.B get
and
.B list
remain available. Because no form writes a file, XDG_CONFIG_HOME and HOME stay
assignable. No setting can turn restricted mode off. A shell started restricted
by
.B \-r
or the name rbash ignores and removes a
.B KOSHCONF
environment variable with a warning. The configuration files are read before
the restrictions take effect. They apply only presentation and diagnostic
options. They warn about the mood, every semantic option,
editor.history.file_path,
and editor.history.max_entries. A configuration file cannot choose
the startup files, the evaluation rules, the command resolution, or a history
path. A child shell or a fresh evaluator inherits the restrictions.
.PP
A sourced script retains the restrictions. A shell script found through PATH
and executed as a command runs without them.
.SH SHELL GRAMMAR
The shell accepts the POSIX Shell Command Language and the Bash extensions
described below. The
.B sh
mood disables Bash-only syntax except for documented compatibility forms.
Compatibility differences are listed under
.BR COMPATIBILITY .
.PP
Constants may be folded and an unreachable command may be removed before the
script runs. The init clause of a C-style for loop still runs once when the
condition folds to zero.
.PP
An if command with no selected branch and no else command returns status zero.
This rule also applies at the end of a sourced file.
.SS Coprocesses
.EX
coproc [NAME] command [redirections]
.EE
.PP
Every mood but
.B sh
accepts a coprocess. The command runs asynchronously and two pipes connect
it to the shell. NAME[0] is read from, NAME[1] is written to, and NAME_PID holds
the process id. The default name is COPROC.
.PP
A word before the command identifies the coprocess only when a compound command
follows that word. In
.B coproc NAME cat
the word NAME is the first word of a simple command and the coprocess takes the
default name. The launch itself returns status zero.
.SH QUOTING
Single quotes preserve every enclosed byte. Double quotes retain parameter,
command, and arithmetic expansion. A backslash quotes the next byte where the
grammar permits it.
.PP
The form $'...' interprets C escapes including \en, \et, \exHH, and \eOOO. The
form
$"..." marks a string for translation. Every mood accepts it, and it behaves as
an ordinary double-quoted string when no catalog is present. It is recognized
only outside double quotes. Within a double-quoted string, $" is a literal
dollar followed by the closing quote. Under an sh or dash shebang, analysis
reports it as a bash extension absent from POSIX sh.
.SH PARAMETERS
.SS Positional parameters
An unquoted $@ or $* is subject to field splitting. The form "$@" preserves one
field per positional parameter. The form "$*" joins the parameters with the
first byte of IFS. In the sh mood, as in dash, a prefix or suffix removal on
$@ or $* matches the parameters as one string whose pieces are the parameters,
so "${@%%?}" can drop later parameters and "${@#?}" shortens only the first.
In the sh mood a $@ that becomes one string, as in an assignment, a case word,
or a here-document, joins the parameters with the first byte of IFS, or with
nothing when IFS is empty, as dash does. The bash moods join it with a space.
In the sh mood, as in dash, $@ and $* are always set, so "${@\-d}" and
"${@+p}" never test for missing parameters. The colon forms ${@:+p} and any
test of a quoted $* or of a single string treat the parameters as null when
they join to an empty string. The colon forms \-, =, and ? in a field context
treat them as null only when there are none or there is one empty parameter.
A quoted form that expands to no field, such as "${@\-d}" without parameters,
still gives one empty field.
.SS Indexed arrays
An indexed array is assigned with a=(x y z). An element is assigned with
a[2]=z. The form a+=(w) appends elements. An opening parenthesis directly after
a nonempty value, as in a=x(y) or a=\e<(y), is a syntax error in every mood.
The list holds words, so a nested parenthesis, a control operator such as ;
or |, or a redirection operator inside it is a syntax error, as in Bash.
${a[i]} takes an arithmetic subscript.
${a[@]} and ${a[*]} list elements. ${#a[@]} counts elements. ${!a[@]} lists
indices. A negative index counts from the end. Sparse indices remain absent
from ${!a[@]}.
.PP
Inside double quotes, "${a[@]}" preserves one field per element, also when an
operator changes each element or the form sits in the word of another
expansion, as in "${u:-${a[@]#x}}". The form "${a[@]@A}" yields the words of
the declaration.
"${a[*]}" joins elements with the first byte of IFS. An unquoted ${a[@]} or
${a[*]}, with or without an operator, joins elements with the first byte of IFS
and splits the result, so a non-whitespace IFS keeps empty fields. Under an
empty IFS the elements stay apart, except that a prefix or suffix removal joins
them with a space into one word. An unset array read through
${a[@]} is an error under
.BR "set \-u" .
.SS Associative arrays
The command
.B declare \-A m
declares an associative array. The form m[key]=v assigns an element. The form
${m[key]} expands to it. The form ${!m[@]} lists keys. The command
.B unset 'm[key]'
removes one element. A word in the list m=(k1 v1 k2 v2) is not split into
fields and not matched against files, and a quoted "${a[@]}" joins into one
word. A quoted ] inside a key, as in m['k]x']=1, does not close the subscript.
.PP
The iteration order of the keys of an associative array is unspecified and may
differ from Bash. The shell lists them in the order the keys were first
assigned, and an element removed and assigned again moves to the end. A script
that depends on an order should sort the keys.
.PP
The
.B export
and
.B declare \-x
commands on an indexed or associative array keep the array, set element 0 or
key 0 when given a value, and record the export attribute, which
.B declare \-p
prints as \-ax or \-Ax and
.B ${a@a}
reports as x. The commands
.B "export \-n"
and
.B "declare +x"
remove it. As in Bash, no child process receives the array in its environment.
.SH EXPANSIONS
.SS Parameter expansion
The form ${v:offset:length} selects a substring. A negative offset requires a
leading space, as in ${v: \-3}. Without the space, ${v:\-3} is the default-value
form. A negative length excludes that many characters from the end.
On an indexed array, ${a[@]:offset:length} starts at the first element whose
index is at least the offset and takes length elements. A negative offset
counts back from one past the highest index, and one that falls before index
zero selects nothing. On an associative array the offset counts from one in
the listing order, as bash counts it.
.PP
The form ${v/pat/rep} replaces the first match. The form ${v//pat/rep} replaces
every match. The anchors /# and /% select the start and end. The
.B patsub_replacement
shopt option is enabled by default. While it is enabled, an unquoted ampersand
in the replacement denotes the matched text, and so does an ampersand that comes
from an unquoted expansion. A quoted ampersand and the form \e& denote a literal
ampersand. When the option is disabled, every ampersand is literal.
.PP
The forms ${v^^}, ${v,,}, ${v^}, and ${v,} change case with an optional pattern.
The transforms ${v@Q}, ${v@E}, ${v@A}, ${v@P}, and ${v@a} produce a quoted
value, expanded escapes, an assignment, a prompt expansion, or attributes.
A value or key with a byte that does not print in the locale, such as a
control byte or invalid UTF-8, takes the $'...' form in ${v@Q}, ${v@A},
${a[@]@K}, printf %q, declare \-p, and export \-p, so the printed text
evaluates back to the same bytes. The forms ${v@U}, ${v@L}, and ${v@u} apply
the corresponding case conversion.
Any other operator after @ on a set parameter is a bad substitution that ends
the script, and on an unset one it expands to nothing.
.PP
The runtime warning for an unset variable that expands to empty, such as the
one for ${1}, also prints in the sh mood, where dash is silent.
.PP
The form ${!ref} expands the variable named by ref. The forms ${!pre*} and
${!pre@} list names beginning with pre.
.PP
An unquoted tilde that starts the word of a parameter expansion expands, as in
${v\-~} and ${v#~/x}. It stays literal in double quotes and after the anchor of
${v/#~/x}.
.PP
When ${v:\-word}, ${v:+word}, or ${v:=word}, with or without the colon, sits
in double quotes or a here-document, the word follows double-quote rules.
Single quotes in it stay literal, so "${v:\-'a'}" expands to 'a' with its
quotes and "${v:\-\e'a\e'}" keeps both backslashes. A double-quoted part of the
word loses its quotes, and a backslash before } is removed. Outside the sh
mood a backslash inside that double-quoted part is removed before any byte, so
"${v:\-"\ex"}" expands to x, while the sh mood keeps it as dash does. The
expression of an arithmetic expansion treats such a word as a here-document
does, so a single quote or $'...' in it is a syntax error. A $'...' form in
the word is decoded inside double quotes but kept literal in a here-document.
The sh and bash-posix moods also keep it literal inside double quotes, and end
the word at the first } even inside single quotes. The message of ${v:?word}
removes the quotes, as Bash does. A pattern word of #, %, or / still treats
quotes as quoting.
.SS Arithmetic expansion
Arithmetic accepts the C operators, ++, \-\-, **, the conditional operator, the
comma operator, and assignment operators. Literals may use 0x hexadecimal, 0
octal, 0b binary, or a base prefix such as 16#ff. A variable value is
re-evaluated as an arithmetic expression. Nested subscripts such as a[b[c]] are
resolved from the inner expression outward. An empty (( )) command returns one.
An empty $(( )) expansion produces zero. Outside the sh mood, double quotes in
the text of $(( )), (( )), a C-style for header, a subscript, or a substring
offset are removed before the evaluation, as Bash removes them, so
$(( "1 + 2" * 3 )) is 7. Quotes inside a variable value, a single quote, and a
double quote in a
.B let
operand stay an error. The sh mood rejects every quote, as dash does.
.PP
Outside the sh mood, $[expression] is the obsolete Bash spelling of
$((expression)). It ends at the ] that balances its brackets, so $[ a[1] + 1 ]
is one expansion, and it expands, nests, and fails as $(( )) does, inside
double quotes, here-document bodies, and the words of a parameter expansion
too. Inside double quotes a double quote ends the string even within $[, as in
Bash, so "$[ "3" ]" fails as an unterminated expansion and abandons the line.
The sh mood keeps $[ literal, as dash does. Analysis reports the spelling
as SC2007.
.PP
An arithmetic error in the bash and bash-posix moods abandons the rest of the
input line, as Bash does. A $(( )) expansion, a subscript, a substring offset,
and an assignment to an integer variable stop every command left on that line,
including the commands of a function, a loop, or a group it runs inside, and
$? is 1 on the next line. Neither set \-e nor an ERR trap acts on it. The line
ends at an unquoted newline, so the rest of a command that spans lines goes
with it. An eval, a sourced file, a subshell, and a command substitution are
lines of their own: an eval or a sourced file goes on with its next line, and
a subshell or command substitution ends with status 1. A pipeline stage or an
asynchronous command fails alone. An eval or a sourced file inside a
subshell, a command substitution, a pipeline stage, or an asynchronous command
passes the error on, so that subshell ends with status 1 as Bash's does. An
error in an indexed array subscript or in an assignment to an integer variable
goes further: it passes through every eval and sourced file and abandons the
line of the outermost script, and it ends a \-c string or a command
substitution with status 1, while a $(( )) expansion inside the subscript
stays an ordinary arithmetic error. An
error in the expression of (( )),
.BR let ,
a [[ ]] operand, or a C-style for header fails that command with status 1, and
an error in a here-document body fails the command it feeds, unless the error
comes from a $(( )) expansion or a subscript inside it.
.PP
A $(( )) error outside a here-document body ends a non-interactive shell in the
bash-posix mood with status 1 and in the sh mood with status 2, as Bash in POSIX
mode and dash do. It passes
through an eval and a sourced file. A subshell and a command substitution end
with it alone, with status 1 in the bash-posix mood and 2 in the sh mood. An
interactive shell abandons the line instead. In the kosh mood every arithmetic
error ends the script with status 1, like any other evaluation error, while an
eval, a subshell, or a command substitution holds it to a status of 1.
.PP
Other expansion errors follow Bash 5.3 the same way. A glob that matches
nothing under failglob abandons the line in the bash and bash-posix moods. A
bad substitution, such as ${x!y}, ${x:}, or ${%}, and an assignment to a
read-only variable by NAME=value, NAME=(...), NAME+=value, NAME[i]=value, or
${NAME:=value} abandon the line in the bash mood and, like a $(( )) error, end
a non-interactive shell in the bash-posix and sh moods. A read-only
${NAME:=value} leaves status 2 in the bash mood. A read-only for loop variable
fails the loop in the bash mood and ends a non-interactive shell in the
bash-posix and sh moods. A read-only prefix assignment is reported and skipped
in the bash mood, so its command still runs, while the bash-posix mood
abandons the line, or ends the shell before a special builtin. An unset
parameter under set \-u and ${NAME?word} end the script in every mood, also
inside a function substitution, which ends the enclosing subshell. In
the kosh mood each of these errors ends the script.
.PP
Outside the kosh mood, an unset parameter under set \-u, ${NAME?word}, or a
bad substitution in a here-document body fails only the simple command the
body feeds, with status 1 in the bash and bash-posix moods and 2 in the sh
mood, as Bash and dash do. In the sh mood a $(( )) error in the body fails the
command the same way. The error still ends the shell before a special
builtin, and before a function in the bash and bash-posix moods, as in Bash.
.PP
In the sh mood, an error of a regular builtin, such as a missing cd
directory or an invalid test operand, fails that command with status 2 and
the script goes on. An error of a special builtin or of local, such as shift
past the last parameter or export of an invalid name, ends a non-interactive
shell with status 2 unless command runs it. A bad trap signal only fails trap,
and extra operands of exit, break, and continue are ignored, as in dash. Run
through command, local checks its operands and binds nothing, even outside a
function, so it succeeds. read without a variable, printf with an option or
without a format, ulimit with a bad number or a second operand, and wait or
jobs with an unknown job number fail with status 2. times prints the seconds
with six decimals. In every mood printf without a format fails with status 2,
as Bash and dash do.
.PP
The
.B interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers
koshconf option uses arbitrary-precision integers and accepts finite base-10
decimal literals. The default mood always holds it on, and the other moods start
with it off. When the option is
disabled, signed 64-bit arithmetic uses wrapping operations. Addition and
subtraction use the larger operand scale. Multiplication adds the operand
scales. Division and remainder use the larger operand scale and truncate toward
zero.
.PP
A standalone expression with decimal integer literals on both sides of ^ may
report that ^ is bitwise XOR and suggest ** for exponentiation. Expressions
that contain names, assignments, or another bitwise operator do not produce
this suggestion.
.SS Command substitution
The forms $(command) and `command` replace a command with its output. A command
substitution restores runtime state, shell options, directory stack, working
directory state, and file creation mask when it finishes. Trailing newlines are
removed from the output. The bash and sh moods also drop every null byte, as
Bash and dash do, and the kosh mood keeps them. A syntax error inside
a command, process, or function substitution is reported at its place in the
outer source, and a script or command string containing one does not run. Every
such error in the source is reported. The
same check covers substitutions in double quotes, here-document bodies, and the
words of a parameter expansion or arithmetic expansion, and the subscript of
an array assignment. A substitution nested
more than 64 levels deep is reported as a syntax error, as it is at run time.
.PP
Analysis diagnostics apply to the commands inside command, process, and
function substitutions, and to substitutions in parameter expansion words,
arithmetic expansions, here-document bodies, arithmetic commands, and C-style
for headers. The inner findings use the file's own line and column. The body of
a command, backtick, or process substitution is analyzed as a subshell, so a
variable it assigns is not visible after it, and a read inside it counts as a
read of the parent variable. A function substitution shares the current shell.
A backtick body whose escapes change its text is not analyzed.
.SS Process substitution
The forms <(command) and >(command) supply a descriptor path. The command runs
in a separate process. A form expands anywhere in an unquoted word, so
p=<(command) assigns the path and x<(command) joins it to the text around it.
The word of an unquoted ${v:\-word}, ${v:+word}, ${v:=word}, or ${v:?word}
expansion, with or without the colon, expands a form outside quotes in the
word. A pattern or replacement word keeps the form as text.
The path stays open until the command that expanded it finishes, and a path in
the word list of a for loop stays open until the loop finishes. The shell then
closes it and waits for the process, while Bash leaves the process running.
A process substitution sets
.B $!
to its process. The shell remembers its status as it remembers the status of
a job, and
.B wait
with that process returns it, waiting first when the process still runs.
.B wait
without an operand waits for the process that
.B $!
identifies the process when it is a process substitution.
A later command cannot open the path. An
.B exec
command without a program keeps the process for as long as its redirections
hold the descriptor, closes the path, and reaps the process after it ends
without waiting for it. On POSIX the path is /dev/fd/63 or the next free
descriptor above it. On Windows the path identifies a named pipe that behaves
like a FIFO. A program may open or inspect the path before it performs a read
or a write.
The path may be opened again. Each reader continues where the previous reader
stopped, a reader that opens the path after the stream ends receives end of
file,
and each writer appends to the same stream. A redirection that finds the pipe
busy retries for up to two seconds. When a reader that consumes input in large
blocks
closes the path in the middle of a line, the next reader may receive that whole
line again.
.SS Brace expansion
The form {a,b,c} produces alternatives. The forms {1..10}, {a..z}, and
{1..10..2} produce ranges. A numeric range such as {001..10} retains its width.
Braces nest. A lone {a} remains literal.
.SS Field splitting
IFS controls field splitting. A run of an IFS whitespace byte collapses. A run
of a non-whitespace IFS byte produces an empty field where applicable.
.SS Pathname expansion
The
.B extglob
option enables ?(), *(), +(), @(), and !() matching. The shell parses these
groups as part of a word in every mood whatever the option, so @(a)<(cmd) is one
word where Bash with the option off reports a syntax error. The pattern of
.B [[
always matches these groups, as in Bash, whatever the option. The
.B globstar
option enables **. The
.BR dotglob ,
.BR nullglob ,
and
.B failglob
options control leading dots, nonmatches, and failed matches. In the default
mood, a nonmatching glob is fatal. In a compatibility mood with failglob
disabled, the nonmatching pattern remains literal. The warning policy may
report that literal fallback. A glob in command position is fatal in the
default mood and reports a warning in a compatibility mood unless diagnostics
are disabled.
.PP
A nonempty
.B GLOBIGNORE
holds colon\-separated patterns. A pathname expansion result that matches one
pattern is removed, and a glob whose results are all removed follows the
nonmatching rules. A pattern is matched against the whole result. A star in a
pattern crosses a slash only at the end of the pattern. The names . and .. are
never results while GLOBIGNORE is nonempty. A nonempty assignment enables the
.B dotglob
option, an empty assignment leaves it unchanged, and an unset of GLOBIGNORE
disables it. A value imported from the environment filters nothing, and
.B dotglob
is left alone until the shell assigns GLOBIGNORE. The
.B extglob
and
.B nocaseglob
options apply to the patterns.
.PP
The character locale decides how much a ? matches. The locale is selected by
the first nonempty value of LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE, and LANG. These variables are
read from the shell at the time of each match, and an assignment, an unset, an
export, and a prefix assignment all take effect. A value whose codeset after
the dot is UTF\-8 or utf8, in any letter case, selects UTF\-8 when the platform
can select that locale. Any other value selects bytes, and so do a UTF\-8 name
that the platform cannot select, an empty value, and no value. Bash falls back
to the C locale in the same cases.
.PP
In UTF\-8, a ? and each step of a * consume one whole valid UTF\-8 sequence,
and an invalid byte counts as one character. In bytes, each of them consumes
one byte. In UTF\-8, ${#v} and ${v:offset:length} count characters, ${v^},
${v,}, and ${v~} change the case of multibyte letters, and a ${v/pat/rep} whose
pattern holds an invalid UTF\-8 sequence matches by byte. The rule applies to
pathname expansion, case, [[ == ]], ${v#pat}, ${v%pat}, ${v/pat/rep},
${v^pat}, and compgen \-X.
.PP
In UTF\-8, a bracket expression consumes one whole character, its members and
range endpoints are whole characters, a range compares code points, and a
negated bracket still consumes one character. A POSIX class such as [:alpha:]
tests a non\-ASCII character against the Unicode class of the platform, and it
matches nothing on a platform that has no such class. In bytes, a bracket
compares one byte. In UTF\-8, an extglob group splits the subject only at
character boundaries, and its inner patterns follow the same rule. Completion
always matches by UTF\-8 sequence.
.SH REDIRECTIONS
The operators &> and &>> redirect standard output and standard error to one
file. The operator |& pipes both streams. The operator <<< supplies a
here-string followed by one newline. A descriptor number written before <<<
or <<, as in 7<<< or 8<<EOF, sets the descriptor that receives the input.
.PP
A redirection of the form {var}>, {var}<, {var}<<<, or {var}<< allocates a
descriptor numbered ten or greater and assigns its number to var, so analysis
counts var as assigned. The form {var}>&\- closes the descriptor whose number
var holds.
.PP
An appending redirection written inside a loop body opens its file once and
keeps that descriptor for the rest of the loop. The same file and descriptor
number are reused on every iteration, and the descriptor is closed when the loop
ends. Up to sixteen such descriptors are kept at a time, and a loop that appends
to more files than that reopens the ones beyond the limit on every iteration. A
descriptor is held across the iterations whether the command stands alone or
runs as a pipeline stage. Writes therefore continue to the original file even
after that file is renamed or removed. Bash reopens the file on every iteration.
.PP
A diagnostic inside a tab-stripped here-document carries no source caret.
.PP
In the bash moods, the redirections of a program, of a command with no word,
and of a subshell expand as they do in Bash, in a child process. A program
started through
.B command
is included. An assignment such as ${v:=x}, an arithmetic side effect, or a
function substitution in the word of such a redirection or in the body of its
here-document leaves no change in the shell afterward. The redirections of a
builtin, a function, a command group, and
.B exec
expand in the shell itself, and so do the redirections of every command in the
kosh mood.
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
Command lookup checks functions, builtins, cached program paths, PATH, and
bundled koshkit commands when the active mood and options enable them. Running a
command or using the
.B hash
builtin caches its resolved program path. A change to the contents of any PATH
directory clears every cached path.
.B hash \-r
forgets cached command locations.
.B hash \-R
forgets them and rebuilds the PATH command index at once.
.PP
In an interactive shell, a single directory path in command position changes
the working directory as if it were an operand to
.BR cd .
A script is never affected, as in bash.
Functions, builtins, programs, and bundled utilities take precedence. The
.B autocd
shopt option controls this behavior and is enabled by default in every mood.
When the command is made only of dots, two dots move to the parent directory
and each additional dot moves one more parent directory. This shorthand also
requires a single command word, and functions, builtins, programs, and bundled
utilities keep precedence over it.
.PP
A command path ending in a directory separator fails with status 126 when the
typed target is not a directory. Components are resolved in order. An
unavailable path is reported through its first unavailable component. The caret
covers that component.
.PP
A script without a recognized executable format is run by the shell itself. It
receives the command environment and the command argument zero. The caller's
variables, functions, and traps are not inherited. Each complete top-level
command runs before the next one is parsed.
.PP
PIPESTATUS is updated after every foreground command. It contains the status of
each pipeline stage. When
.B pipefail
is enabled, a pipeline returns the status of its rightmost failing stage.
A pipeline stage that expands to no command, such as
.BR "p=1 | cat" ,
is an error in the kosh mood. The other moods run it as an empty command
that applies its redirections and returns 0.
.SH FUNCTIONS
A function has its own positional parameters and local variables. Call stack
state is exposed through FUNCNAME, BASH_SOURCE, BASH_LINENO, and BASH_COMMAND.
.PP
A function called from a guarded and-or operand or a negation inherits that
condition. The
.B errexit
option remains suppressed throughout the guarded function body.
.PP
In the default mood the RETURN trap fires at the boundary of every function
body. The
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods fire it for a body that the
.B functrace
option follows and for a body that installs its own action. A sourced file
fires the trap in all of these moods. The
.B sh
mood rejects the RETURN condition.
.SH BUILTINS
The shell provides the POSIX and Bash builtins listed below. The standard forms
follow the active compatibility mood unless a difference is stated here.
.PP
With no operand,
.B builtin
groups the names under POSIX, BASH, and Koshka headings. The Koshka section
contains assimilate, bench, koshconf, koshkit, and z. The headings are colored
when
standard output is a color terminal.
.PP
.BR : ,
.BR . ,
.BR [ ,
.BR alias ,
.BR bg ,
.BR break ,
.BR builtin ,
.BR cd ,
.BR command ,
.BR complete ,
.BR compgen ,
.BR compopt ,
.BR continue ,
.BR declare ,
.BR dirs ,
.BR disown ,
.BR echo ,
.BR eval ,
.BR exec ,
.BR exit ,
.BR export ,
.BR false ,
.BR fc ,
.BR fg ,
.BR getopts ,
.BR history ,
.BR jobs ,
.BR kill ,
.BR let ,
.BR local ,
.BR mapfile ,
.BR newgrp ,
.BR popd ,
.BR printf ,
.BR pushd ,
.BR pwd ,
.BR read ,
.BR readarray ,
.BR readonly ,
.BR return ,
.BR set ,
.BR shift ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR source ,
.BR test ,
.BR time ,
.BR times ,
.BR trap ,
.BR true ,
.BR type ,
.BR typeset ,
.BR ulimit ,
.BR umask ,
.BR unalias ,
.BR unset ,
and
.BR wait .
.SS Shell-specific builtins
.TP
.BI "assimilate " TARGET
The running executable is copied through scp to TARGET and installed as
.B kosh
in the first usable remote PATH directory. A path containing sbin is skipped.
/usr/local/bin and $HOME/.local/bin are preferred when they occur in PATH. The
.BI \-\-link\-mood= MOOD
option requests a relative bash, dash, sh, or kosh link. It may be repeated and
accepts comma-separated values. A candidate directory is skipped when a
requested link path already exists.
.PP
The
.BR \-x " or " \-\-trace
option prints remote progress. The
.BI \-\-ssh\-command= COMMAND
and
.BI \-\-scp\-command= COMMAND
options replace ssh and scp. COMMAND is split at literal spaces.
.PP
The uploaded candidate is validated before the target is replaced. A failure
handled by the transaction restores the previous file or symlink and removes
transaction files. Transactions run one at a time. The next transaction
recovers an interrupted transaction. A failure before the remote transaction
starts leaves the installed target unchanged. An unusable partial upload may
remain.
.TP
.B bench
Commands are sampled through
.BR "kosh \-c" .
The wall time, peak resident set, and available performance counters are
reported as a mean, standard deviation, and range. Later commands are compared
with the first. Sampling stops after a nonzero status unless
.B \-\-ignore\-exit\-code
is present. The
.BR \-\-show\-output ,
.BR \-\-no\-shell ,
.BR \-\-runs ,
and
.B \-\-duration
options control output, direct execution, sample count, and the millisecond
budget.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-extended ,
each command also reports user and system time, voluntary and involuntary
context switches, minor and major page faults, block input and output
operations, read and write system calls, and bytes read and written. The
times, switches, faults, and block counts cover the sampled process and the
children it waited for. The call and byte counts cover the sampled process
alone, so
.B \-\-no\-shell
measures the command itself. Read and write counts are available on Linux.
Windows reports a single page fault total, and it has no switch or block
counts. A row the platform cannot report is omitted, never printed as zero.
Without the option no extra rows or system calls are added.
.TP
.BI "koshconf create " "PRESET " "\fR[\fB\-\-force\fR]"
.PD 0
.TP
.BI "koshconf set " "OPTION VALUE " "\fR[\fB\-\-persist\fR]"
.TP
.BI "koshconf get " OPTION
.TP
.BR "koshconf list " "[\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR]"
.TP
.BI "koshconf load " ENCODED
.TP
.BR "koshconf save " "[\fB\-\-persist\fR]"
.PD
The koshconf builtin is the only interface to the settings Koshka owns.
.B koshconf list
prints every option with its current value.
.IP
.B create
writes the default file of the
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash ,
or
.B sh
preset to the user configuration file that
.BR kosh (5)
describes. It refuses to replace an existing file unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.B set
changes the option in the current session. With
.BR \-\-persist ,
it also rewrites that option's line in the user configuration file, through a
temporary file and a rename, and leaves every other line in place. Every
option a file may hold can be persisted; see
.BR kosh (5).
The value is checked before
the session changes, so a value that the file cannot hold changes nothing.
.B get
prints the current value.
.B list
prints a heading row that starts with a number sign and then every option in
alphabetical order as
.IR name = value ,
one line each. It shows a value that a file cannot hold in the
.B $\(aq...\(aq
form. With
.B \-a
or
.BR \-\-all ,
each option that has a Bash spelling is followed by a second line that carries
its alias with the same value: legacy.posix.NAME for a POSIX
.B set \-o
option, otherwise legacy.bash.NAME. The set-only options
.B hashall
and
.B nolog
appear there as legacy.posix.hashall and legacy.posix.nolog, which
.B list
shows for information only. The
.B emacs
and
.B vi
spellings of editor.base_mode are not listed, because they stand for two
values of one option. Only
.B list
accepts
.BR \-\-all .
.B load
applies a value of
.B KOSHCONF
to the current session.
.B save
prints the current settings as the value of
.BR $KOSHCONF :
the same encoding received by every spawned process, which holds the mood
and the interactive options. A kosh started with
.B KOSHCONF=$(koshconf save)
adopts them, and
.B load
accepts the value. With
.BR \-\-persist ,
.B save
instead writes every setting that a file may hold, with its current value, to
the user configuration file through the same temporary file and rename as
.BR create ,
and prints nothing. A restricted shell refuses
.B save \-\-persist
and allows a plain
.BR save .
.IP
A boolean is written as on or off and is also read as true, false, 1, or 0. An
enumeration is written by name. A string must be valid UTF-8, and
editor.history.max_entries is a decimal integer from 0 to 2147483647. A negative
number is an operand rather than a flag, so it reaches the value check. An
unknown option or value, and a file that
cannot be read or written, is an error with status 1, and a malformed form
exits with status 2. An unknown option name or form suggests a close name when
one exists. An interactive shell reports the file that
.BR create ,
.BR "set \-\-persist" ,
or
.B save \-\-persist
wrote on standard error; a script stays silent. Only
.B set
and
.B save
accept
.BR \-\-persist ,
and only
.B create
accepts
.BR \-\-force .
.TP
.B z
The current directory is changed to one selected from the frecency store,
which records each directory change of an interactive shell. A single operand that is an existing directory is selected as it is.
Otherwise the operands form one case-insensitive substring query. An entry
equal to the query wins, then an entry whose last component equals it, then
any entry that contains it. Within each of these, visit count and recency
determine the ranking. With no operand, the home directory is selected.
.SS Compatibility forms
.TP
.B dirs
With no operand, the builtin prints the current directory followed by the saved
stack. A +N operand prints the Nth entry from the top, and a \-N operand prints
the Nth entry from the bottom. The
.B \-v
form keeps the selected entry's full-stack index.
.TP
.BR "pushd \-n DIR" ", " "popd \-n"
The first form adds DIR below the current directory, and the second removes
the entry below it. Neither changes the current directory.
.TP
.B set
With no option and no operand, the shell variables are printed as sorted
assignments. A minus enables an option. A plus disables it. The forms
.BR "set \-o NAME" " and " "set +o NAME"
select the option by its Bash name. Only Bash names are accepted. The OPTION
REGISTRY section lists them. With no NAME,
.B set \-o
prints current states and
.B set +o
prints reusable commands, both in name order. A long option such as
.B \-\-mood
is rejected with a pointer to
.BR koshconf .
The
.BR \-E ,
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-T ,
and
.B \-B
forms control errtrace, history expansion, physical directory resolution,
functrace, and brace expansion. The
.B \-N
form controls the exit code diagnostic that
.B \-\-show\-all\-exit\-codes
enables at startup, and it reports a successful zero as well. The
.B \-t
form and the
.B onecmd
option stop input after one top-level command. The Koshka forms
.BI "set \-M " MOOD
and
.BI "set \-L " MOOD,...
select the mood and source the startup files of other moods with the same mood
table as the command-line options. Without a value,
.B set \-M
prints the active mood name and
.B set \-L
prints the initialized moods. The
.BR \-I ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-S ,
and
.B \-G
letters set interpreter.mimic_shebang, debug.print_syntax_tree,
debug.print_lexed_word_escapes, debug.report_every_exit_code,
debug.print_evaluation_statistics, and
debug.print_memory_report_at_exit. Every other Koshka setting belongs to
.BR koshconf .
The
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-h ,
and
.B \-m
letters are accepted without effect. The
.B \-k
form places unquoted assignment arguments in the command environment and
removes them from the argument list. The
.BR \-W ,
.BR \-WW ,
and
.B \-WWW
forms set optimizer.warning_level.
.PP
The
.B history
option records submitted interactive commands. The
.B histexpand
option expands interactive history references. The supported references are
.BR !! ,
.BR !\-N ,
.BR !N ,
.BR !prefix ,
.BR !?text? ,
and
.BR !# .
An event reference accepts the word designators
.BR :^ ,
.BR :$ ,
.BR :* ,
.BR :N ,
.BR :N\-M ,
.BR :\-M ,
.BR :N* ,
.BR :N\-$ ,
and
.BR :N\- .
The colon may be omitted before
.BR ^ ,
.BR $ ,
.BR * ,
and
.BR % .
The
.B %
designator selects the word matched by the most recent
.B !?text?
search, including a search on an earlier input line.
An event or word reference accepts the pathname modifiers
.BR :h ,
.BR :t ,
.BR :r ,
and
.BR :e .
They select the directory, final component, name without its final suffix, and
final suffix. The
.BI :s/ old / new /
modifier replaces the first literal match.
.B :gs
and
.B :as
replace every match, while
.B :Gs
replaces the first match in each word. The delimiter after
.B s
may be any byte, and its final occurrence may be omitted at the end of the
input line. An unescaped ampersand in the replacement inserts the matched text.
The
.B :&
modifier repeats the previous substitution, including one from an earlier
input line. The
.BR :g& ,
.BR :a& ,
and
.B :G&
forms apply the corresponding scope. An empty old text reuses the previous
substitution text. It reuses the latest
.B !?text?
search text when no previous substitution exists.
.PP
The
.B :q
modifier quotes the selected text as one shell argument. The
.B :x
modifier splits the text at blanks and newlines and quotes each resulting
argument. The
.B :p
modifier prints and records the expanded input without executing it. A leading
.BI ^ old ^ new ^
performs the first substitution on the previous event. The final caret may be
omitted. Text after the final caret remains in the expanded input, and a
modifier may follow it directly.
.PP
Single-quoted and backslash-escaped references remain literal. A reference
immediately before a closing double quote also remains literal. As in bash, a !
right after [ stays literal when a ] follows later on the line, so [!a] keeps
its negation, a ! right after ${ stays literal when a } follows later on the
line, so ${!name} stays an indirect expansion, and $! stays a parameter. A
comment
marker at the start of a word prevents expansion through the end of the line.
An event, word, modifier, or substitution error prevents execution and history
recording and preserves the previous exit status. Both history options are
enabled by default in an interactive shell and disabled in a noninteractive
shell.
.PP
Each shell loads one private history branch at startup. An interactive shell
appends submitted commands to the shared file immediately without importing
commands written by other running shells. A noninteractive shell never writes
the file on its own. Its stored events, deletions, clears, imports, and
.B fc
edits change only the private branch, and only
.B "history \-w"
and
.B "history \-a"
reach the file. Recall, expansion, listing, and editing use the private
branch. A command that duplicates the preceding private event is skipped. A
multiline command is stored as one event, and its embedded newlines are
preserved. KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE bounds retained and stored history and defaults to
4096. A value of zero disables history retention and storage. A history file
that exists and cannot be read is reported once at startup, and the session
begins with an empty history.
.PP
The
.B ignoreeof
option requires consecutive end-of-file input before an interactive shell
exits. Its threshold is read from
.BR IGNOREEOF .
Enabling the option sets an unset IGNOREEOF to 10. Assigning IGNOREEOF enables
the option. Disabling the option or unsetting IGNOREEOF disables it. The
.B nolog
option is accepted for Bash compatibility and does not change execution.
.TP
.B "time [\-p|\-\-posix] [\-R] command [argument ...]"
The command or pipeline is timed. The
.B \-p
and
.B \-\-posix
options select the POSIX report. Under the bash mood the report is a blank line
followed by three tab separated rows in the minutes form. The default mood
indents the same three rows and adds a processor busy row. The default report
includes the peak resident set size when the platform supplies a nonzero value.
The
.B \-R
option always adds the same row, including when TIMEFORMAT selects a custom or
empty report. Under the
.B sh
mood the name is not a keyword and is resolved on the command search path.
.TP
.B enable
Every builtin remains enabled. The
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.B \-s
compatibility flags are accepted without effect. The
.B \-a
option lists every builtin.
.TP
.B hash
Resolved command locations are remembered or listed. The
.B \-r
option clears the cache. The
.BI "\-p " path
option records path as the location of each name. The
.B \-t
option prints the remembered location of each name, preceded by the name and a
tab when several are given, and fails for a name with no remembered location.
.TP
.B "history [\-cnrwaps] [\-d selector] [operand]"
.br
.B "history {\-S|\-\-sync}"
Without a maintenance option the stored events are listed, and a numeric
operand limits the listing to that many newest events. The
.B \-r
option loads the history file into the list, and an operand specifies a file
whose events join the list without being written to the history file. The
.B \-n
option also loads the history file, and an operand specifies a file whose
already read bytes are skipped. The
.B \-w
option writes the list to the history file, and an operand specifies a file that
is replaced. Without an operand it atomically replaces the shared file with
this shell's private branch. The
.B \-a
option writes the list to the history file, and an operand specifies a file that
receives the events this shell has not written to a named file yet. The written
events are limited by
.BR KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE .
Without an operand,
.B \-a
appends the events of the private branch that the file lacks, such as the
events a noninteractive shell stored or an operand of
.B \-r
supplied.
The
.B \-S
and
.B \-\-sync
forms explicitly replace the private branch with a locked snapshot of the
shared history file and accept no operand.
The
.B \-c
option clears the private branch and leaves the history file intact, as bash
does, and
.B \-p
prints its operands without storing them. The
.B \-d
option deletes an event number, a negative offset from the newest event, or an
inclusive event range. An interactive shell also removes a deleted event from
the history file through an atomic replacement. The
.B \-s
option joins its operands with spaces and stores the result as a history event.
.TP
.B "fc [\-e editor] [\-lnr] [first [last]]"
The selected history events are edited and executed in the current shell. A
decimal selector specifies an event number. A negative selector is relative to
the current history position. A text selector finds the newest event with that
prefix. The first and last selectors form an inclusive range. Without
selectors, editing selects the previous event.
.PP
The
.B \-l
option lists events without executing them. A list without selectors contains
up to the previous sixteen events. The
.B \-n
option omits event numbers, and the
.B \-r
option reverses the selected order.
.PP
The editor is selected by
.B \-e
and then by FCEDIT and EDITOR. The default is vi, or ed in POSIX mood. A dash
as the editor skips editing and executes the selected event. The editor receives
the temporary file path as its final argument.
.PP
The form
.B "fc \-s [old=new ...] [command]"
selects one event and applies each literal replacement in order. The result is
printed to standard error and executed. The active fc event is replaced by the
executed command. The return status is the status of the executed command.
.TP
.B bind
Flags and operands are accepted without changing key bindings. The builtin
returns zero.
.TP
.B caller
Without an operand, the builtin returns one and prints nothing at top level. It
returns zero inside a function or sourced file. A numeric operand selects a
call frame and prints its line number and source file. A nonnumeric operand is
an error.
.TP
.B help
Without a pattern, builtin names are printed one per line. A builtin name
renders the same help as its
.B \-\-help
flag. An unknown name is an error. The
.B \-d
option prints the name and short description. The
.B \-s
option prints only the usage synopsis. The
.B \-m
option prints manpage-style name, synopsis, description, and option sections.
Each mode accepts more than one builtin name.
.TP
.B logout
A login shell runs the EXIT trap and exits. An optional operand supplies the
status. A non-login shell reports an error and returns one.
.TP
.B suspend
The shell stops on SIGSTOP and resumes on SIGCONT. A login shell rejects the
operation unless
.B \-f
is present.
.TP
.BR "declare, typeset"
An indexed array is declared by
.BR \-a ,
an associative array by
.BR \-A ,
an arithmetic integer by
.BR \-i ,
lowercase conversion by
.BR \-l ,
read-only assignment by
.BR \-r ,
uppercase conversion by
.BR \-u ,
and an exported name by
.BR \-x .
The plus forms remove the integer and case-conversion attributes. Lowercase
and uppercase conversion cancel each other when both are requested.
The
.B \-f
and
.B \-F
options restrict the operands to functions, printing each recorded definition or
only its name. The
.B \-p
option prints the matching declarations, and an indexed array lists each set
element under its own index. Without operands, the
.B \-p
option or an attribute option lists every variable that carries the requested
attributes. The
.B \-n
option makes the name a reference to the variable its value selects, which may
carry an array subscript. A read, an assignment, an append, an element
assignment, an export, and an unset of the reference act on that variable, and
a chain of references is followed. A for loop over a reference binds the
reference to each word, ${!name} gives the name the reference reaches, and
.B "unset \-n"
removes the reference itself and leaves a name that is not a reference alone.
The conditional [[ \-R name ]] and, outside
the sh mood, test \-R name and [ \-R name ] are true for a reference with a
value. A reference with no value binds to the name its
first assignment gives it. A reference to itself is an error at the top level,
and a circular chain counts as unset with a warning and fails an assignment.
Through a circular chain, read, printf \-v, and export fail with status 1,
arithmetic, a prefix assignment before a builtin, and declare warn and skip
the write, a prefix assignment before eval, a sourced file, a function, or an
external command warns and binds a plain exported variable of that name until
the command ends, an element
or array assignment warns and turns the reference into an array, and unset
removes the reference. When a function's reference loops back to its own name,
an assignment warns and sets the variable the function's local hides.
Inside a function, local \-n name=name or declare \-n name=name warns and
leaves the name reaching the variable outside the function, as Bash falls back
to the global one, though a caller's local of that name is reached first. Reads
and writes act on that variable, declare \-p and \-R report a reference,
${!name} abandons the line, and unset in the same function turns the name into
an unset local. declare \-gn name=name in a function makes a circular
reference. A first assignment of its own name to a reference with no value
makes the same self reference when the reference is local to the running
function, and a circular reference otherwise. A write through a
reference applies the read-only, restricted, integer, and case rules of the
target, and readonly or declare \-r on a reference marks the target. A target
must be a name or a name with a subscript, which is evaluated on each use. A
reference to an element accepts only a scalar write. An array literal, an
append of one, an element assignment, read \-a, and mapfile through it fail,
and the failing assignment abandons the line. An export of a value through it
assigns the element and reports an error with status 0. A scalar assignment to
an array or an associative array sets element 0 or key 0, and an export of an
array records the export attribute that declare \-p prints as \-x, as in Bash.
An array carrying the attribute never reaches a child environment. A
read-only name, PATH, IFS, and a dynamic variable other than RANDOM and SECONDS
cannot become a reference. As in Bash, RANDOM and SECONDS take the attribute,
a value given with it seeds or sets them as an assignment does, and they keep
their generated value, which is never a name. An expansion or an assignment
statement of the name then abandons the line, a write by read, printf \-v, or
declare fails with status 1, [[ \-v name ]] is false, and ${!name} gives the
generated value. unset leaves the name alone,
.B "unset \-n"
removes it with its special behavior, and
.B +n
restores it. A local of either name in a function is an ordinary variable that
may become a reference. The
.B +n
form removes the reference and keeps its value. The
.B \-g
and
.B \-t
options are accepted without effect.
.TP
.B local
The name is local to the running function. The
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-u ,
and
.B \-x
attributes have the same meanings as for declare, so
.B "local \-n"
passes a caller's variable by reference. A
.B \-
operand saves the
.B set
options, and the function return restores them; the restricted option stays
as the function left it.
.TP
.B readonly
Each named variable is marked read-only. The
.B \-p
option lists the read-only variables in a reusable form. The
.B \-a
option treats each name as an indexed array, and
.B \-A
treats each name as an associative array. The
.B \-f
option marks each named function read-only. A read-only function cannot be
redefined and cannot be unset. A bare
.B \-f
lists each read-only function with its body.
.TP
.BR "mapfile, readarray"
The
.B \-t
option stores input lines in an array without their delimiters. The
.B \-n
option limits the line count, and
.B \-s
discards leading lines. The
.B \-O
option selects the first array index. The
.B \-d
option replaces the newline delimiter with the first byte of its value. The
.B \-u
option takes input from a file descriptor. The
.B \-C
option evaluates its callback with the destination index and current record as
arguments. The callback runs after each quantum of records and before the
current record is assigned. The
.B \-c
option sets that quantum. The default quantum is 5000 records.
.TP
.B printf
The
.B \-v
option assigns the formatted result to a variable.
The numeric locale is selected by the first nonempty value of LC_ALL,
LC_NUMERIC, and LANG in every mood. These variables are read from the shell at
each call, and an assignment, an unset, an export, a local, and a prefix
assignment all take effect. A floating operand is read with the decimal point
of that locale, and an operand that is not consumed whole is an invalid number.
The floating conversions print that decimal point. The
.B \(aq
flag groups the digits of the
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR u ,
.BR f ,
.BR F ,
.BR g ,
and
.B G
conversions with the thousands separator of the locale. The C and POSIX
locales, no value, and a locale that the platform cannot select use a dot and
no grouping, and so does every locale on Windows. Arithmetic expansion does
not use the locale.
.TP
.B read
The
.B \-r
option retains backslashes. A plain read treats a trailing backslash as a line
continuation. The
.B \-a
option splits the line into a named indexed array. The
.B \-d
option replaces the newline delimiter, and an empty value selects a NUL byte.
The
.B \-n
option bounds the byte count, and
.B \-t
bounds the wait in seconds. The
.B \-p
option prints a prompt at a terminal, and
.B \-s
keeps terminal input unechoed. The
.B \-u
option takes input from a file descriptor. The
.B \-q
option takes one byte and returns zero for y or Y. The
.B \-e
option is accepted without effect. The line editor is always used at a
terminal.
.TP
.B compgen
The
.BI "\-V " varname
option stores the candidates in the specified indexed array and writes nothing
to standard output. An invalid name is a usage error. An associative or
read-only
target is rejected. A generator that matches no candidates leaves the array
empty and returns one. The
.BI "\-P " prefix
and
.BI "\-S " suffix
options add text to each candidate after word prefix matching and
.B \-X
filtering.
.TP
.B complete
A registered spec offers the candidates of its actions, its
.B \-G
glob, its
.B \-W
word list, and, on an explicit Tab, the COMPREPLY of its
.B \-F
function and the output lines of its
.B \-C
command, in that order. The
.B \-C
command runs as a command substitution with the command name, the word, and
the previous word appended as quoted arguments, and with COMP_LINE,
COMP_POINT, COMP_KEY, and COMP_TYPE exported. Each nonempty line of its output
is one candidate, taken without word prefix matching. The ghost suggestion
never runs a function or a command, never expands the glob, and never adds
directory names. Of the actions it offers only those the shell answers from
memory, so the user, group, hostname, service, command, file, and directory
actions wait for a Tab. A
.B \-I
spec that has none of the generators the ghost uses leaves the ghost of the
command names in place. The actions
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-j ,
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-v ,
and
.BI "\-A " action
list what
.B compgen
lists for the same action, and the glob keeps the matches that start with the
word. The
.B \-X
filter then removes candidates using the rules of
.B compgen \-X
does, and
.B \-P
and
.B \-S
add their text to each remaining candidate. The
.B \-o dirnames
option adds the directory names that start with the word when nothing else
matched, and
.B \-o plusdirs
adds them always. A spec with a directory or file action or a glob ends each
candidate that refers to a directory with a slash unless its function runs
.BR "compopt +o filenames" ,
and dirnames and plusdirs mark the directories they add after the function.
As in Bash, a spec quotes the special characters of the candidates it inserts
only when it generates file names, through
.BR "\-o filenames" ,
a directory or file action, a glob, or directories that dirnames and plusdirs
added.
.B \-o fullquote
quotes every candidate, and
.B \-o noquote
leaves file names unquoted. It also leaves an empty filename-completion result
unquoted when that result falls through, unless fullquote is on.
.B \-o nosort
keeps the candidates in the order the spec generated them and drops only a
candidate equal to the one before it.
.B \-o nospace
inserts no space after an accepted candidate whatever
completion.add_space_after_completed_word chooses.
A dash candidate is offered only when the word starts with a dash. An empty
result falls through to the next completion source, except that with
.B \-o bashdefault
and without
.B \-o default
it stays empty as in Bash unless the word starts with $ or ~ or holds a glob
character. A single candidate that is not a directory closes a quote the word
left open. An unknown
.B \-o
option name or
.B \-A
action name is an error with status 2, and nothing is registered.
.IP
With
.B progcomp_alias
on, a command with no spec of its own uses the spec of the first word of its
alias. An explicit Tab also follows a command found through PATH to the name
of its symlink target. The ghost suggestion follows only the alias, so it
never searches PATH. A command with no spec reached this way uses the
.B \-D
default spec, which generates candidates using those rules. When its function
returns 124, completion restarts with the spec the function registered for the
command. The
.B \-E
spec serves an explicit Tab on a line that holds nothing, not even a blank,
and the
.B \-I
spec serves the command word in place of the command names, including an
empty line with no
.B \-E
spec. When either offers nothing, the completion stays empty unless the spec
has
.B \-o default
or
.BR "\-o bashdefault" ,
which fall back to the command names. Their functions and commands receive
.B _EmptycmD_
and
.B _InitialWorD_
as the command name. Given more than one of
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-E ,
and
.BR \-I ,
the first in that order is used and command names are ignored.
.IP
The
.B \-p
option prints each spec with its options, actions, and arguments in the order
Bash prints them, including an argument given as an empty string, followed by
the
.BR \-I ,
.BR \-D ,
and
.B \-E
specs. The
.B \-r
option removes the specified specs or the given slot, and every spec when given
neither. A name or slot with no spec is an error.
.TP
.B compopt
The
.BI "\-o " option
form turns a
.B complete \-o
option on and
.BI "+o " option
turns it off, and off wins when both refer to the same option. The change
applies
to the spec of each named command, or to the
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-E ,
or
.B \-I
spec, the first in that order, in place of the names. Inside a running
completion function with neither, it applies to the current completion only,
and the registered spec keeps its options. Without
.B \-o
or
.BR +o ,
the options are printed as one
.B compopt
line for each spec, with the command name or the slot flag last, and the
current completion prints under its command name. An unknown option name or
option letter is an error with status 2 that changes nothing. A name or slot
with no spec is an error with status 1, and the other names still change.
Outside a completion function, a call with no name and no slot is an error
with status 1.
.TP
.B type
The
.B \-V
option prints everything the shell holds about each name. A function is reported
with the file and the line its definition starts on, followed by the body as the
shell recorded it. A function defined by an eval is reported as defined in
.B eval
on its line within the eval text. A builtin and a bundled utility are reported
with their description. A keyword and an alias hold nothing further. The
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-P
options keep their terse output and ignore
.BR \-V .
The
.B \-a
option applies the longer form to every resolution it prints. Under the bash
moods a function is named a function and its body is printed from the plain
form. Every other mood treats the command as a shell function and prints
nothing after it.
.SH KOSHKIT UTILITIES
The
.B koshkit
builtin runs a bundled utility. An unknown utility is an error. Every utility
supports
.BR \-\-help .
A utility invoked through a matching executable name also supports
.BR \-\-version .
The
.B koshkit \-\-list
form prints the utility names one per line.
.PP
With no utility,
.B koshkit
groups the catalog under POSIX and Koshka headings. The Koshka section contains
the additional inspection and reporting utilities. The headings are colored
when standard output is a color terminal.
.PP
The bundled set contains basename, bc, cal, calc, cat, chgrp, chmod, chown,
cksum, cmp, comm, cp, csplit, cut, date, df, diff, dirname, du, env, evil,
evildisk, evilfiles, evilfs, evilio, eviliso, evillogs, evilnet, evilps, evilss,
expand, expr, file, find, flock, fold, fuser, getconf, goodcore, goodfsw,
goodnode, goodstat, grep, head, id, killall, link, ln, locale, logger,
logname, ls, make,
man, mkdir, mkfifo, more, mv, nice, nl, nohup, nproc, od, paste, pathchk,
pkill, pr, printenv, ps, readlink, realpath, renice, retry, rm, rmdir, sed, seq,
sleep, sort, split, stat, strings, stty, sync, tabs, tail, tee, timeout, touch,
tput, tr, tsort, tty, uname, unexpand, uniq, unlink, watch, wc, which, who,
whoami, xargs, and yes.
.PP
The command
.BI "koshkit \-\-assimilate " DIR
installs one symlink per utility into DIR. When direct koshkit lookup is
enabled, a PATH program takes precedence over a bundled utility. The shell
started under a utility name runs that utility. Started as
.BR koshkit ,
the link that
.B make install
places next to
.BR kosh ,
it runs the utility specified by its first operand, such as
.BR "koshkit ls \-l" ,
and otherwise acts as the koshkit builtin, so
.B koshkit \-\-list
lists the utilities. Either form skips the shell startup: it loads no settings
or startup file and ignores KOSH_FLAGS.
.TP
.B basename
The final path component is printed. An optional suffix operand is removed.
.TP
.B cal
A Gregorian month or year is printed. Calendar output starts each week on
Sunday. Month titles and weekday headings are colored on a color terminal.
Weekend dates are red, and the current date is emphasized when it appears.
The
.BR \-a ", " \-\-today
option starts each week on Monday and prints the current date in words below
the calendar.
.TP
.B calc
Arithmetic operands are evaluated. The
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
and
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pipe
options read expressions interactively or from a pipe.
Arbitrary precision integers and finite decimal values are accepted in every
mood, and the
.B interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers
option does not narrow a calc result. The abs, atan, ceil, cmp, cos, exp,
fact, fib, floor, frac, gcd, int, iseven, isint, isodd, length, lcm, ln, max,
min, scale, sgn, sin, and sqrt functions are available.
.TP
.B bc
Expressions, assignments, functions, arrays, control flow, input and output
bases, and scale are supported. The
.B \-l
option enables atan, cos, exp, ln, and sin and selects a scale of 20.
.TP
.B cat
Files or standard input are concatenated. The
.B \-n
option numbers lines. The
.B \-u
option is accepted because output is never delayed. The
.B \-\-syntax\-highlighting
option colors recognized shell source when standard output is a terminal. A
file containing a null byte is written without highlighting. Line numbering
continues across file and standard input boundaries.
.TP
.BR chgrp ", " chown
The group, or the owner and optional group, of each file is changed. The
.B \-R
option recurses, and
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-L ,
and
.B \-P
select how symbolic links are followed. The
.B \-h
option changes a link instead of its target. The
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
option stops a recursion from descending into a directory on a file system
other than the one of its operand. The directory itself is still changed.
.TP
.B chmod
Symbolic or octal modes are applied to each file. The
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recursive
option changes directories and their contents without following symbolic
links. The
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
option skips every directory below an operand that is on a file system other
than the one of that operand, together with its contents. It has no short form
because
.B \-x
is a mode operand.
.TP
.B cp
Files and directory trees are copied. The
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-v
options are supported. A recursive copy keeps symbolic links as links. The
.B \-L
option follows every symbolic link, and
.B \-H
follows only a link named on the command line. The
.B \-P
option restores the default and copies a link as a link without
.BR \-r .
A copy without
.B \-r
or
.B \-P
follows links. A symbolic link that leads back into a directory being copied is
reported as cyclic and skipped, and the status is 1. The
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
option copies a directory on a file system other than the one of its source
operand as an empty directory, without its contents.
.TP
.B df
Mounted filesystems or the filesystems of the operands are reported. The
.B \-k
and
.B \-P
options select 1024-byte blocks and the POSIX header. The
.BR \-h ", " \-\-human\-readable
option prints sizes in powers of 1024 with K, M, G, T, and P suffixes under the
header
.BR "Filesystem Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on" ,
and the
.BR \-H ", " \-\-si
option does the same in powers of 1000, with a lowercase k for the kilo suffix.
.TP
.B diff
Two files are compared line by line. The
.BR \-\-color [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
option colors removed lines red, added lines green, hunk headers cyan, and the
unified file headers bold. WHEN is auto, always, or never, and a bare option
means auto. Output is never colored without the option. The
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-a ,
and repeated
.B \-L
options select unified output, ignore whitespace, force text input, and set the
unified header labels. Up to two labels may be supplied. An unlabeled filesystem
header includes the file modification time. Equal files return zero, different
files return one, and usage or input errors return two.
.TP
.B dirname
The directory portion of one path is printed.
.TP
.B du
Every file and directory below each path is printed in descending size order.
Symbolic links are not followed. Output is colored on a color terminal. The
.B \-s
option prints only each path total. The
.B \-h
option prints human-readable sizes. The
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
option skips every entry on a file system other than the one of its path
operand. The
.B \-\-tree
option prints the same entries as a tree with box-drawing branches. Children are
sorted by size, largest first, then by name, and each line shows the size
column, the branch, and the last path component, with each path operand shown
as written. With
.BR \-s ,
only the operand lines are printed. The
.BR \-T ", " \-\-top\-largest
option prints a human-readable tree of only the largest entries and every
ancestor of one up to its operand. The tree takes at most 36 lines, or the
terminal height minus four, at least ten, when that is smaller. Names are
colored by file type as in
.BR ls .
With no path, the current directory is used.
.TP
.B env
Temporary assignments are applied before a command, or the environment is
printed. The
.B \-i
option starts with an empty environment, and each
.B \-u
option removes one variable.
.TP
.B xargs
Arguments read from standard input are appended to a command. The
.B \-0
option splits the input on null bytes, and
.B \-r
skips the command when the input is empty. The
.B \-E
option sets the end-of-input marker, and
.B \-I
replaces a string in the command with each input line. The
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.B \-s
options limit each command to a number of lines, arguments, or bytes. The
.B \-p
option asks before each command runs, and
.B \-t
traces each command. The
.B \-x
option fails when a command does not fit in the size limit.
.TP
.B od
Input bytes are written in the formats selected by
.BR \-t .
The
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.B \-x
options select the o1, c, u2, o2, d2, and x2 formats in command line order.
.TP
.B printenv
The whole environment is printed when no name is given. Named values are
printed in operand order. The status is one if any name is absent.
.TP
.B file
Filesystem object kinds and file contents are classified. The default content
tests recognize more than 100 executable, archive, document, data, image, audio,
and video formats. The
.BR \-h ", " \-\-no\-dereference
option classifies a symbolic link. The
.BR \-L ", " \-\-dereference
option follows it. The
.BR \-i ", " \-\-regular\-only
option skips content tests. The
.BR \-m ", " \-\-magic\-file
option adds a magic database, and
.BR \-M ", " \-\-magic\-only
uses only the named databases.
.TP
.B find
Sorted trees are walked with
.BR \-name ,
.BR \-iname ,
.BR \-type ,
.BR \-maxdepth ,
.BR \-mindepth ,
.BR \-print ,
and
.BR \-print0 .
The
.BR \-xdev " and " \-mount
primaries are always true and keep the walk from descending into a directory on
a file system other than the one of its starting point. The directory itself is
still matched. The
.B \-exec
action runs a command that ends at a
.B ;
argument for each matched path, replacing each
.B {}
with the path. A command ended by
.B +
after
.B {}
receives the matched paths in batches. A failed command skips the later actions
for that path.
.TP
.B flock
A command runs while a directory lock is held. The
.B \-\-transaction\-held\-lock
option keeps the lock after the process that launched the transaction exits.
.TP
.B fuser
Process IDs that use each named file are printed. The
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.B \-u
options are supported.
.TP
.B getconf
System and string variables take one name. Path variables also take a path.
An undefined or unsupported variable prints
.BR undefined .
A platform query failure prints a diagnostic and returns a nonzero status. The
.BR \-a ,
.B \-\-all
option prints every known variable as a name and value, using the current
directory for path variables unless a path is supplied. The
.BR \-v ,
.B \-\-specification
option selects a supported POSIX environment.
.TP
.B grep
Regular-expression matches are printed. The
.BR \-\-color [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
option colors matches bold red, file names magenta, line numbers green, and
separators cyan. WHEN is auto, always, or never, and a bare option means auto.
When color is on, GREP_COLORS holds colon-separated GNU capabilities such as
.BR ms=01;31 ,
with ms, mt, sl, cx, fn, ln, se, and rv taking effect and an unknown name
ignored. Parsing stops at the first character of a value that is not a digit or
a semicolon, and an empty value turns that color off. The deprecated GREP_COLOR
sets the match color below GREP_COLORS. Matches are not highlighted with
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-l ,
or
.BR \-q .
Output is never colored without the option. The
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.BR \-h
options ignore case, invert matches, recurse into directories, show line
numbers, and suppress file-name prefixes. The
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
option makes
.B \-r
skip every directory on a file system other than the one of its operand. It has
no short form because
.B \-x
matches whole lines. The combined
.B \-rnh
form is accepted, and options may follow the operands.
A pattern is a basic regular expression. The
.B \-E
option treats patterns as extended regular expressions and the
.B \-F
option treats them as fixed strings. The two options conflict and exit with
status 2.
Each
.B \-e
option adds a pattern, and each
.B \-f
option adds the lines of a file as patterns. When either option is given, every
operand is a file. A newline inside a pattern separates two patterns, and a line
is selected when any pattern matches it. An empty pattern matches every line,
and an empty pattern file matches none.
The
.B \-x
option selects only lines that match entirely.
The
.B \-c
option prints the count of selected lines for each file, prefixed with the file
name when several files are searched. The
.B \-l
option prints each file with a selected line once, and takes precedence over
.BR \-c .
The
.B \-q
option prints nothing and exits with status 0 at the first selected line, even
if an earlier file could not be read. The
.B \-s
option suppresses messages about files that cannot be read, and the exit status
is still 2. The standard input is named
.I (standard input)
in prefixes, counts, and lists. The exit status is 0 when a line is selected, 1
when no line is selected, and 2 on an error. The locale is chosen from the first
nonempty value of
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.BR LANG .
In a UTF-8 locale, a dot and a bracket expression match one whole character.
In every other locale, they match one byte.
.TP
.B head
The first ten lines are printed by default. The
.B \-n
option selects a line count and the
.B \-c
option selects a byte count.
.TP
.B tail
The last ten lines are printed by default. The
.B \-n
option selects a line count and the
.B \-c
option selects a byte count. The
.BR \-q ,
.BR \-\-quiet ,
and
.B \-\-silent
options never print file name headers, and the
.BR \-v ,
.B \-\-verbose
options always print them. The
.B \-f
option keeps each regular file open after the initial output and prints the
bytes appended to it, with a header whenever the output switches to another
file. The
.B \-F
option follows the file name, reports and reopens a file that is replaced,
removed, or created later, and implies
.BR \-\-retry .
The
.BI \-\-follow= descriptor|name
option selects the mode, and a name follow without
.B \-\-retry
stops following a removed file. A truncated file is reported on standard error
and read again from its start. Standard input is followed only when it is a
regular file, and a pipe is printed once. The
.BR \-s ,
.B \-\-sleep\-interval
option sets the longest wait between checks in seconds, and the wait is one
second by default. The
.BI \-\-pid= pid
option ends the follow after that process exits. On Linux and macOS, the
follow is woken by file changes, and on other targets, the files are checked
every interval. An interrupted follow ends with status 130.
.TP
.B killall
Processes are signaled by exact name. The signal is selected and the signal
names are listed with
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-signal ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-\-list .
.TP
.B pkill
Processes whose names match a pattern are signaled. The signal is selected and
the signal names are listed with
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-signal ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-\-list .
.TP
.B ln
Hard links are created by default. The
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-L ,
and
.B \-P
options create a symbolic link, remove an existing destination first, follow a
symbolic link source, and link a symbolic link source itself.
.TP
.B logname
The login session user name is printed. It can differ from the effective user
reported by
.BR whoami .
.TP
.B ls
Sorted entries are listed with
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-h .
The
.B \-F
option appends a type indicator to each name, and
.B \-p
appends a slash to each directory. The
.B \-d
option lists a directory operand as itself. The
.B \-n
option prints numeric owner and group identifiers, and
.B \-g
and
.B \-o
print the long form without the owner or without the group. The
.B \-k
option is accepted because sizes are never scaled. The
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-S ,
and
.B \-r
options sort by modification time, by size, and in reverse. The
.B \-R
option lists every subdirectory that is reached, and
.B \-\-tree
prints an indented directory tree. The
.B \-L
option sets the maximum recursion depth. The
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
option keeps
.B \-R
and
.B \-\-tree
from descending into a directory on a file system other than the one of its
operand, and the directory is still listed. It has no short form because
.B \-x
lists entries across. Names are colored by file
type when the standard output is a terminal. The
.B \-\-color
option accepts always, auto, and never.
.TP
.B make
Compact make rules are run. The
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-\-file ,
.BR \-C ,
and
.B \-\-directory
options are supported. The
.B \-B
option always rebuilds targets, and
.B \-k
continues independent goals after an error. The
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-q ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.B \-t
options provide the POSIX make execution modes.
The
.B \-j
extension accepts an optional decimal job count and propagates it through
MAKEFLAGS.
Common variable functions include
.BR call ,
.BR eval ,
.BR filter ,
.BR filter-out ,
.BR foreach ,
.BR patsubst ,
.BR shell ,
.BR sort ,
and
.BR wildcard .
.TP
.B mkdir
Directories are created with
.B \-p
and an octal or symbolic
.B \-m
mode. A symbolic mode is applied to mode 777 with the chmod grammar.
.TP
.B mknod
FIFO, character, and block special files are created with POSIX type operands
or the modern
.B \-\-type
,
.B \-\-fifo
,
.B \-\-character
,
and
.B \-\-block
options. Device nodes accept
.B \-\-major
and
.B \-\-minor
numbers; the
.B \-m
option sets the permission mode.
.TP
.B mv
Sources are renamed with
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B nproc
The available logical processor count is printed. The
.B \-\-all
and
.B \-\-ignore=COUNT
options are supported.
.TP
.B ps
Processes are listed with
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-u ,
and
.BR \-x .
The
.B \-w
option is accepted without effect.
.TP
.B readlink
The target of each symbolic link is printed on its own line. A file that is not
a link is reported, the remaining files are still read, and the status is 1.
The
.B \-n
option suppresses the trailing newline of a single file and is ignored for
several.
.TP
.B realpath
The canonical absolute form of each path is printed.
.TP
.B rm
Paths are removed with
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
Root, dot, and dot-dot are refused. The
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
option skips a directory on a file system other than the one of its path
operand, reports it as being on a different device, and makes the status 1.
.TP
.B rmdir
Empty directories are removed. The
.B \-p
option also removes empty parent directories.
.TP
.B seq
Signed integer sequences are printed with a nonzero step.
.TP
.B sleep
Execution pauses for summed durations with optional s, m, h, or d suffixes.
.TP
.B sort
Input lines are sorted in byte order, or by the keys specified with
.BR \-k .
A key is
.IR F[.C][bdfinr][,F[.C][bdfinr]] .
The
.BR \-t
option sets the field separator byte. The
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.B \-r
options ignore leading blanks, compare only blanks and alphanumeric characters,
fold lowercase letters, compare only printable characters, compare leading
numbers by value, and reverse the result. The
.B \-u
option writes the first of each run of equal keys. The
.B \-c
option checks that the input is sorted and reports the first line out of
order, and
.B \-C
checks it without a message. The
.B \-o
option writes the result to a file after all input is read. The
.B \-s
option skips the last-resort comparison of whole lines, and
.B \-m
is accepted for merging sorted input.
.TP
.B stty
Terminal settings are reported or changed. The
.B \-a
option reports all settings. The
.B \-g
option reports an encoded state that can be supplied later to restore the
same state.
.TP
.B tee
Standard input is copied to output files. The
.B \-a
option appends.
.TP
.B timeout
A command runs with a time limit. The duration accepts a suffix. The
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-signal ,
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-\-kill\-after ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-\-preserve\-status
options select the signal, send KILL after a further duration, and return the
command status.
.TP
.B touch
File times are updated. The
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-r ,
and
.B \-t
options select the access time, suppress creation of a missing file, select the
modification time, specify a reference file, and set an explicit timestamp.
.TP
.B tr
Input bytes are translated with ranges and character classes. The
.B \-d
option deletes bytes. The
.BR \-c
and
.B \-C
options replace the first set with every byte outside it, in byte order. The
.B \-s
option squeezes each run of a repeated byte from the last set into one byte.
.TP
.B uniq
Adjacent equal lines are collapsed. The
.B \-c
option prints run counts. The
.B \-d
and
.B \-u
options write only lines that have a repeat or only lines that have none. The
.B \-f
and
.B \-s
options ignore leading fields and characters when lines are compared. A second
operand is the output file.
.TP
.B unlink
One non-directory path is removed.
.TP
.B evil
System identity, uptime, processor, memory, swap, root filesystem, process,
filesystem, and network information is printed in titled sections called
Identity, Session, Accounts, Resources, Limits, Activity, and Anomalies, each
with its name and value rows indented two spaces beneath the title. A section
without rows is omitted. The
.B \-a
option adds system details that are omitted from the default report. The
additional activity counters are shown only when the platform provides them;
pressure-stall totals are measured in microseconds. On Linux, this includes
counts of the current process's namespace entries and cgroup memberships;
each reports unavailable if its procfs source cannot be read. The
.B \-s
option selects identity fields. The
.B \-u
option prints every local user account. The
.B \-\-color
option accepts always, auto, and never.
.TP
.B goodcore
A running process can be captured with
.BR \-p .
An existing core can be packaged with its executable through
.BR \-b .
Mapped libraries and host metadata are included. Zstandard compression is used
when available, with gzip as the fallback.
.TP
.B eviliso
Isolation evidence is grouped into namespace, cgroup, login-session, remote
socket, container-runtime, container, and Kubernetes sections. With no
selector, all sections are shown. The
.B -a
or
.B --all
option includes detailed rows, including per-process namespace attribution,
controller membership, login times, and remote peers. The
.B namespace
section groups visible process relationships by namespace type and numeric
identifier and prints their process counts. With
.BR --all ,
it prints type, identifier, process ID, process name, and self/other role for
each relationship. A relationship that cannot be read or disappears during
collection is retained with an
.B unavailable
identifier. The
.B cgroup
section prints hierarchy, controller, path, process ID, process name, and
self/other role columns. With
.BR --all ,
it adds each membership of another process that has the same hierarchy,
controller, and path as one of the current process's memberships. Sessions are
printed as user and terminal rows. With
.BR --all ,
each row also includes its login time; an unavailable timestamp is shown
explicitly. The
.B \-\-remote
option selects a remote-peer table with protocol, state, queues, local and peer
endpoints, and socket identifiers. With
.B \-\-all ,
the same rows add process, owner, network namespace, orchestrator, runtime,
container, and cgroup columns. Command lines, process names, and cgroup
memberships are shortened to keep the table usable. A dash marks information
that disappeared or could not be read.
Otherwise
.BR -n , -c , -s ,
and
.B -r
select namespaces, cgroups, sessions, and remote sockets; the
.B \-k
option selects container-runtime evidence. The long-only
.B \-\-container
and
.B \-\-kubernetes
selectors select only their corresponding sections.
The plural
.B \-\-containers
spelling is an equivalent alias for
.BR \-\-container .
Selectors combine, and only the selected sections are printed.
The remote section starts with a socket summary of remote and total socket
counts.
.PP
The runtime section prints runtime, source, and evidence columns. Evidence is
a cgroup path or an existing container marker file such as
.BR /.dockerenv .
With
.BR \-\-all ,
it adds process ID, process name, and self, other, or host role columns.
The container section prints runtime, container identifier, and process count
columns. With
.BR \-\-all ,
each process has its own row with process ID, name, role, and cgroup columns
in place of the count. The Kubernetes section prints an evidence table with
source, host, namespace, and evidence columns. Evidence comes from the
.B KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST
variable, the service-account namespace file, and
.B kubepods
cgroup components. Values read from the environment or the file are limited to
one line of 256 bytes with control bytes replaced by spaces. A second table
lists workloads with pod UID, QoS class, runtime, container, and process count
columns. The QoS class is
.BR guaranteed ,
.BR burstable ,
or
.BR besteffort .
With
.BR \-\-all ,
the process count is replaced by process ID, name, role, and cgroup columns for
each process.
.TP
.B evildisk
The utility prints capacity and filesystem identity for each named path or
mounted filesystem. It also prints native disk error and retry totals when the
operating system provides them. With
.B \-a
mount labels and UUIDs plus filesystem error counters are included when the
platform exposes them. The same option adds SMART status, model, and protocol
data when smartctl or macOS diskutil is available.
.TP
.B evilfiles
Open files are listed for visible processes in a table titled Open files whose
rows are indented two spaces beneath the title. The
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.B \-t
options filter by process, owner, command prefix, socket descriptor, and terse
process identifier output. On a terminal the command and name columns are
shortened with an ellipsis to fit the width; the
.BR \-w " and " \-\-wide
options keep them whole. Pipes show the FIFO type, and anonymous inodes show
the a_inode type. On Linux a socket shows its family as unix, IPv4, IPv6,
netlink, or packet, and the ENDPOINT column holds the local and remote address
of a TCP or UDP socket, with the TCP state, and the path and peer inode of a
Unix-domain socket.
.TP
.B evilfs
Mounted filesystem sources, targets, types, and options are printed in a sorted
table.
.TP
.B evilio
Current memory, swap, and cumulative disk I/O counters are printed without
waiting. The
.B \-a
option adds processor usage, paging, scheduler state, pressure stalls, disk
activity, and swap rates from a one second sample.
Nonzero stall rates and disk failures are highlighted. Byte sizes and byte
rates use compact binary units by default; the
.BR \-h " and " \-\-human-readable
options request that representation explicitly. The
.B \-\-cumulative
option waits for the specified number of seconds, M, and prints the counter
change over that window; the default window is one second. With
.BR \-\-ps ,
each mode prints per-process data and leaves out a process without I/O; a
process selected with
.B \-p
is printed even without I/O. The
.BR \-l " and " \-\-live
options print the report again and again until interrupted. Their optional
value, N, is the number of seconds between samples and between refreshes; it
defaults to 0.5 seconds.
Output uses
the alternate screen and restores the previous contents on exit.
Every frame of a live utility starts with a header line that identifies the
utility,
shows a LIVE indicator, the local time, the window and the refresh
interval, and, on a terminal, the keys the utility accepts. The header
is shortened to fit the terminal width and is colored only when color is
enabled. A key takes effect at once, without echo and without Enter: q quits
with status 0, and an interrupt quits with status 130. Each frame is written
with one
write and follows the terminal size at the time of the frame. Output
that is not a terminal carries a plain header and no escape sequences.
When both options are present,
.B \-\-live
sets how often the utility samples and refreshes, and
.B \-\-cumulative
sets the length of a rolling window that keeps the counter changes of the last
M seconds. The window never changes the sampling or refresh interval. The
counter columns show the accumulated change inside the window, and a heading
such as
.B READ OPS/2s
indicates its length. A burst stays in the report until its samples age out of
the
window, and then it leaves at once; counters do not fade gradually. When the
window is shorter than the interval, the last interval is divided in
proportionally. A row that is not seen again leaves the report after it has been
missing for a whole window. A process is identified by its identifier and
start time, so a reused identifier begins a new row. The
.BR \-\-ps ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-NUMBER
options otherwise print only lifetime process I/O counters. They show every
process, limit the table, select one process, or limit the table to NUMBER rows.
The
.B \-\-sort
option orders rows by a full metric name or its shortest unambiguous prefix.
Process reports accept
.BR pid ,
.BR read ,
.BR write ,
.BR read-ops ,
and
.BR write-ops .
Disk reports accept
.BR read ,
.BR write ,
.BR read-ops ,
.BR write-ops ,
.BR busy ,
.BR read-latency ,
.BR write-latency ,
.BR average-queue ,
.BR queue ,
.BR errors ,
and
.BR retries .
Metrics sort from largest to smallest, unavailable values sort last, and ties
use the process identifier or device name. Process identifiers sort from
smallest to largest. Busy, latency, and average queue values require a sampled
report. Live reports sort the values from the displayed window.
.TP
.B evillogs
Core dump settings, recognized dump reports, and known log directories are
printed. Each core dump directory and each log directory is a titled table
with its name, entry count, and total size in the title and its rows indented
two spaces beneath it. The
.BR \-\-cores
and
.B \-\-logs
options select one report.
.TP
.B evilnet
Assigned IPv4 and IPv6 addresses are printed in a sorted table. The
.B \-a
option adds interface traffic, packet errors, packet drops, and a TCP counter
table.
.B \-f
prints TCP failure and packet-loss telemetry without interface or socket rows.
Nonzero error, drop, and failure counters are also reported as warnings. The
.BR \-t " and " \-\-traffic
options print only the interface traffic table. On Linux the interface that
carries the default IPv4 route is shown in bold green when color is enabled.
The
.B \-\-live
option samples and refreshes the traffic table every N seconds, defaulting to
0.5 seconds.
The
.B \-\-cumulative
option sets a window of M seconds, defaulting to one second. Without
.BR \-\-live ,
it enables the traffic table, waits M seconds, and prints the counter change
over that window. With
.B \-\-live
it keeps a rolling window of M seconds that never changes the sampling and
refresh interval. The traffic columns show the accumulated change inside the
window, and the headings carry its length, as in
.BR RX\ PACKETS/2s .
The
.B \-\-sort
option orders traffic rows by name, receive or transmit bytes, packet counts,
errors, or drops, using the shortest unambiguous prefix. Numeric counters sort
from largest to smallest, unavailable values sort last, and ties use the
interface name.
.TP
.B goodnode
Inode identity, allocation, known filesystem capabilities, verification state,
and a CRC32C checksum are printed for each path. The
.B \-i
option locates an inode beneath the path selected by
.BR \-r .
The
.B \-\-verify
option runs the platform's native filesystem verification when it supports
it. On APFS it runs
.B diskutil verifyVolume
and reports the command status without parsing localized output.
.TP
.B evilps
Visible processes are printed as a parent and child tree. The
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-U ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-M ,
and
.BR \-w
options show process identifiers, sort numerically, enable every field, show
command lines, show owners, show processor usage, show resident memory, and
keep command lines at full length. Without
.BR \-w ,
a command line is cut to the width of the terminal. Processor usage is
accumulated time in an ordinary report and the average utilization over the
window in a sampled report. The
.B \-\-sort
option accepts name, pid, cpu, memory, or an unambiguous prefix of one of those
names. Each sorted tree starts with its highest-ranked process and shows its
parents and children beneath it. CPU sorting uses the value shown in the report.
The
.B \-NUMBER
operand limits the output to NUMBER processes.
On a terminal, in a report and in a live view, command lines are shortened to
the width of the output; the
.BR \-w " and " \-\-wide
options keep them whole.
The
.B \-C
option is shorthand for
.BR \-\-cumulative .
.BR \-l " and " \-\-live
options sample and refresh the process tree until interrupted. Their optional
value, N, is the number of seconds between samples and between refreshes; it
defaults to 0.5 seconds. On a terminal,
.BR j ,
.BR k ,
and the arrow keys scroll, the page keys scroll by ten lines,
.B g
and
.B G
jump to the top and bottom, and
.B s
cycles the sort key. The
.B /
key starts a search; the typed text is edited with Backspace, Enter applies it,
and Escape clears it. The
.B \-\-cumulative
option sets a window of M seconds, defaulting to one second. With
.BR \-\-live ,
the window rolls over the last M seconds and never changes the sampling or
refresh interval; a window shorter than the interval divides the last interval
in proportion. A new process shows unavailable processor utilization until two
samples have been collected. A process is identified by its identifier and
start time, so a reused identifier begins a new row. Without
.BR \-\-live ,
the utility takes two snapshots M seconds apart and the report shows the
average processor utilization between them.
.TP
.B evilss
Visible TCP, UDP, and Unix-domain sockets are printed in a table. Address
family, protocol, listening state, process identifiers, and the header can be
selected with ss-compatible options. The
.B \-\-live
option, which has no short form because
.B \-l
selects listening sockets, samples the sockets again, and refreshes the same
table every optional number of seconds until interrupted; the default is 0.5
seconds.
.TP
.B goodstat
File metadata is printed as a labeled report. Its type uses the same classifier
as
.BR "koshkit file" .
The
.B \-L
option follows symbolic links.
The
.B \-c
and
.B \-f
options add a CRC32C checksum and filesystem capacity report, respectively.
.TP
.B goodfsw
Path changes are reported by polling filesystem metadata. Machine-readable
output is the default: each record contains the Unix scan time, a numeric event
mask, and the path. The
.BR \-h " and " \-\-human-readable
options use formatted timestamps and descriptive event names. The
.BR \-m " and " \-\-machine-readable
options select the numeric format explicitly. The
.B \-\-timezone
option selects local or UTC time for human output, and
.B \-\-precision
selects zero through nine fractional timestamp digits. The
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-\-event-flags ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.B \-e
options select recursion, timestamp prefixes, appended event names, one
filesystem, one event batch, the polling period, and an excluded substring.
.TP
.B retry
A command is repeated until it succeeds or its attempt limit is reached. The
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-m ,
and
.B \-q
options set the attempt count, initial delay, backoff factor, maximum delay,
and quiet mode.
.TP
.B stat
File or filesystem metadata is printed. The
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-c ,
and
.B \-\-printf
options dereference links, select filesystem data, select terse output, apply a
format, and apply a format without an automatic newline.
.TP
.B sync
Dirty filesystem data is flushed. The
.B \-d
option flushes file data for each operand. The
.B \-f
option flushes the filesystem containing each operand.
.TP
.B watch
A command is run at a fixed interval. The
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-g ,
.B \-e
options set the interval, omit the title, exit after output changes, and exit
after a command error. The
.B \-x
option is accepted for compatibility. The title and screen controls are emitted
only when standard output is a terminal.
.PP
Utilities with styled reports use color when their output stream is a terminal.
Redirected output contains no terminal escape sequences.
.TP
.B wc
Lines, words, characters, and bytes are counted with
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-m ,
and
.BR \-c .
A character is a UTF-8 sequence.
.TP
.B which
Builtins, PATH programs, and koshkit utilities are resolved. The
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-\-all ,
.BR \-q ,
and
.B \-\-quiet
options are supported.
.TP
.B whoami
The effective user name is printed.
.TP
.B yes
The joined operands, or y when none are given, are repeated until output
closes.
.SH LINE EDITING
The interactive editor starts in emacs mode. The command
.B set \-o vi
selects vi mode. The command
.B set \-o emacs
restores emacs mode.
.PP
A line wider than the terminal continues on rows that start under its first
character. When the prompt leaves fewer than 20 columns beside it, those rows
start two columns from the left edge instead.
.PP
When a ghost suggestion is shown at the end of the line, the right arrow and
End accept all of it. Ctrl\-Right and Alt\-F accept only its next word.
.PP
Ctrl\-K, Ctrl\-U, Ctrl\-W, Alt\-D, Alt\-Backspace and Ctrl\-Delete save the
text they erase in a kill ring of 16 entries that lasts for the session, and
consecutive kills join into one entry. Ctrl\-Y inserts the newest entry. Alt\-Y
right after a yank replaces the inserted text with the next older entry and
wraps around after the oldest. Ctrl\-T swaps the character before the cursor
with the one under it, or the last two characters at the end of the line. A
combining mark, an emoji skin tone, a sequence joined by a zero-width joiner,
and a flag move as one character, and Left, Right, Backspace, and Delete step
over them as one character too. The editor counts such a sequence two columns
wide, as a terminal draws it. Alt\-T
swaps the word before the cursor with the word after it, or the last two words
at the end of the line. Alt\-. and Alt\-_ insert the last word of the newest
history entry, and each further press replaces it with the last word of an
older entry. A quoted last word is inserted whole with its quotes. Undo reverts
a yank or a last-word insertion with all of its replacements in one step, and a
Tab completion with the space after it in one step that restores the caret.
.PP
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E opens the line in a temporary file with the editor selected by
VISUAL, EDITOR, or vi. The value is split on blanks without expansion.
When the editor exits with status 0, the saved text replaces the line with its
trailing newlines removed, and nothing runs until Enter is pressed. An editor
that is not found or that exits with another status leaves the line alone and
reports a message above the prompt. An editor that stops on SIGTSTP or
SIGSTOP, such as after Ctrl\-Z, is continued at once, since the line waits for
it to exit. An editor stopped by any other signal, stopped more than 32 times,
or stopped after the shell received an interrupt is killed, and its stop status
is reported. The key
does nothing under
.BR \-T .
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-U undoes the last edit. Ctrl\-X before any other key is ignored,
and that key runs as usual.
.PP
A key that waits for another key before it acts identifies itself and what it
waits for on the hint rows below the input, in place of the command hint, until
the
next key resolves it. The header row lists the keys pressed so far and the
body lists the keys that complete them. Ctrl\-X shows the header
.B "pressed ctrl\-x"
over
.BR "waiting for ctrl\-e (edit in $VISUAL), ctrl\-u (undo)" .
In vi command mode a pending operator shows the keys typed so far and its
motions, such as
.B "pressed d"
over
.B "waiting for a motion: w, b, e, $, 0, ^, d (line), ..."
or
.B "pressed \(dqa2d"
over
.BR "waiting for ..." ,
and so do a pending count, a pending register, the register key
.BR \(dq ,
the find keys
.BR f ,
.BR F ,
.B t
and
.BR T ,
the replace key
.BR r ,
and the colon command line. A key typed fast enough to arrive with the prefix
is read at once without drawing the rows. The rows are the hint rows, so
turning off both
.B editor.hints.show_command_synopsis
and
.B editor.hints.show_live_diagnostics
turns these off too.
.PP
Ctrl\-Z undoes while the editor collects a line, the same as Ctrl\-_. A program
that runs in the foreground has the terminal in its usual mode, so Ctrl\-Z
stops it and the shell reports the stopped job. Ctrl\-Shift\-Z redoes when the
terminal reports it apart from Ctrl\-Z. PROMPT_COMMAND and the expansion of
the prompts run with the terminal in its usual mode, so a program they start
receives plain keys and Ctrl\-C interrupts it. While the editor collects a line,
it asks the terminal for such reports through the disambiguate flag of the kitty
keyboard protocol
.RB ( "ESC [ > 1 u" )
and xterm modifyOtherKeys level 1
.RB ( "ESC [ > 4 ; 1 m" ).
It withdraws both
.RB ( "ESC [ < u" " and " "ESC [ > 4 m" )
whenever it hands the terminal back: before a command runs, around the
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E editor and the external selector, while an explicit completion
runs, and on exit. A shell that dies of SIGABRT, SIGBUS, SIGFPE, SIGILL,
SIGSEGV, or SIGTRAP while the editor holds the terminal also withdraws them and
restores the terminal modes first. Ctrl\-Shift\-Z then arrives as
.B "ESC [ 122 ; 6 u"
or
.BR "ESC [ 27 ; 6 ; 90 ~" ,
and
.B "ESC [ 90 ; 6 u"
and
.B "ESC [ 27 ; 6 ; 122 ~"
also redo. Every other key in either encoding, such as Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-D,
Escape, an Alt key, or a keypad key, is read as the bytes a legacy terminal
sends for it, so each binding acts the same. A terminal that supports neither
protocol ignores the requests. Inside tmux the modifyOtherKeys request takes
effect when the tmux
.B extended\-keys
option is on, and GNU screen discards both. The
.B koshconf set editor.request_extended_key_reports off
command stops the requests from the next prompt on, and the option is on by
default. Without these modes most terminals send the Ctrl\-Z byte for both
keys, and the Windows console reports no shift state for it and receives no
request, so there both keys undo and Ctrl\-^ redoes.
.PP
A control character in the line, a ghost suggestion, or a menu entry is drawn
in caret notation, such as ^[ for ESC and ^? for DEL, and a C1 control as a hex
escape such as \e\ex9b, so it never reaches the terminal raw. A tab and a line
break in the line keep their layout. A file name with a control character
completes in the $'...' form with escapes. A command name, a spec word, or a
noquote file name keeps its other bytes and has each control run spliced in
as $'...', while compgen prints names raw.
.PP
On a non-empty line, the up arrow recalls only the history entries that begin
with the text typed before the first up arrow. Each further up arrow moves to
an older match and the down arrow moves back. Stepping past the newest match
restores the typed text. An empty line steps through the whole history. The
.B koshconf set editor.history.arrow_keys_search_by_typed_prefix off
command turns this off, and the option is on by default.
.PP
Once the cursor is past the command word, the rows directly below the input
show a dimmed hint for the command. The hint identifies the command in the
pipeline
segment, list element, or command substitution under the cursor. A hint is a
header row that identifies its kind and a body
under it. The body is the synopsis of a builtin under
.BR "builtin synopsis" ,
of a bundled koshkit utility under
.B "utility synopsis"
when no PATH program of that name runs first or the word follows
.BR koshkit ,
or of an external program under
.BR "command synopsis" ,
and of a subcommand such as
.B git commit
under
.B "subcommand synopsis"
when its own manual page or help output is known. When the cursor is inside a
flag, the hint uses the flag description under the
.BR flag
header and starts with the flag forms and value name,
such as
.BR "\-f, \-\-file=FILE" .
An alias shows its definition as
.IR name =\(aq expansion \(aq
and then the synopsis of the command it runs, separated by a middle dot, under
.BR "alias synopsis" .
A function shows
.IR name ,
.B defined at
.IR file : line
under
.BR "function synopsis" ,
or its definition on one line when it was defined at the prompt. Every row is
indented by two columns. The header is cut with an ellipsis at the terminal
width, and the body wraps at spaces onto at most three rows, the last of which
is cut with an ellipsis. If the terminal is too short for the input and all
hint rows, the editor drops body rows, then shows only the body on one row,
then hides the hint. The rows are hidden while a completion
menu or history search is open and erased when the line is submitted. A
keystroke uses only data already loaded, and it never runs
.BR man
or the command.
.PP
After a short pause, the editor loads what the hint of an external program
needs: its section 1 manual page through
.BR man ,
a subcommand page such as
.BR git\-commit (1)
among the manual roots that
.BR manpath (1)
reports, and, for a command on the help allowlist, the output of its help flag
and of the subcommand under the cursor. The same trust rules as flag completion
apply, so
.BR man ,
.BR manpath ,
and the program run only from a directory owned by the user or root that no
group or other user can write. Loads run in the background, so typing stays
responsive and the hint updates when the result is ready. A Tab that needs a
load shows a
.B loading...
row while it waits. A key closes the row and is typed as usual, and Escape or
Ctrl\-C closes the Tab quietly. The language server and
.B \-\-debug\-complete\-at
wait for the load instead. A Tab that runs a
.B complete \-F
function or a
.B complete \-C
command shows the
.B loading...
row before it runs. The
.B koshconf set editor.hints.show_command_synopsis off
command turns these hints off and stops the loads, and the option is on by
default.
.PP
In the same pause, a line that parses is analyzed as Enter would analyze it in
the current mood and warning level, and the rows show the wording of the first
finding, such as
.BR "An unquoted variable can split into words and expand globs. (SC2086)" ,
under its severity,
.BR error ,
.BR warning ,
or
.BR note ,
in place of the synopsis until the line changes. This analysis follows no
sourced
file and resolves no command name, so it opens no file and searches no PATH.
The finding and the syntax problem below follow
.BR editor.hints.show_live_diagnostics ,
which
.B koshconf set editor.hints.show_live_diagnostics off
turns off; the option is on by default and is independent of
.BR editor.hints.show_command_synopsis .
.PP
When the line has a syntax problem that running it would report, the same rows
show that problem in place of the synopsis, in the same dim style, under
.BR "syntax error" .
The line is parsed as Enter would parse it in the current mood, and the body
is the first parse error followed by its detail, such as
.BR "Unterminated if, expected 'fi'" .
This covers every error the parser reports, among them an unterminated quote,
an unclosed
.BR $( ,
.BR $(( ,
.BR ${ ,
.BR <( ,
backtick, subshell, brace group, array or
.BR [[ ,
an
.BR if ,
loop, case or function with no closing keyword, a stray
.BR ) ,
.BR ;; ,
.BR fi ,
.BR done ,
.B esac
or
.BR then ,
a pipe or
.BR && ,
.B ||
or
.B |&
with no command on one side, a redirection with no target, a
.B <<
with no delimiter, a
.B case
with no
.BR in ,
a
.B function
with no name, a bad
.B for
variable, and an empty subshell, brace group, loop body, if condition, or then
or else body. The editor submits
the whole buffer on Enter and has no continuation prompt, so an open construct
is an error even when a later line would close it, except after a trailing
backslash, which joins the next row and shows nothing. The error shows with the
caret anywhere, including at the end of the word it starts in. A line over 2048
bytes shows nothing. The problem
follows
.BR editor.hints.show_live_diagnostics .
.PP
The
.B koshconf set editor.auto_close_brackets_and_quotes on
command makes a typed
.BR ( ,
.BR [ ,
.BR { ,
double quote, or single quote insert its closer after the caret. The closer
is added only at the line end or before a blank, a closing bracket, a quote, or
a closer the editor inserted, never after a backslash, and a quote is left
single after a letter or a digit or next to the same quote. Inside a quoted
string, a comment, or a here-document body a
bracket or quote is typed alone, except that
.B $(
and
.B ${
still pair inside double quotes. Typing the closer over an inserted one steps
over it, unless it ends a case pattern, which is typed before the inserted
closer. Backspace between an empty inserted pair deletes both. Any other key,
such as an arrow, keeps the inserted closers as plain text. The option is off
by
default.
.PP
The interactive terminal title shows the current user and directory. While a
foreground program runs, the title shows its command line. Output from
PROMPT_COMMAND or PS1 can replace the idle title.
.PP
RPS1, or RPROMPT when RPS1 is unset or empty, is a right prompt. It is expanded
with PS1 before each prompt and drawn right-aligned on the first input row,
ending one column short of the right edge. Color sequences take no columns.
The right prompt is hidden while the text and ghost on that row would come
within one column of it, while the terminal is too narrow for it, while PS1 is
cut to fit the row, and when its expansion holds a control character other
than a color sequence, such as a line break, a tab, or a title sequence. Rows
after the first input row never carry it. It is never part of the submitted
line, and without
.B editor.transient_prompt_after_submit
it stays in the scrollback after Enter.
.PP
The
.B koshconf set editor.transient_prompt_after_submit on
command makes Enter redraw the submitted line, and Ctrl\-C the interrupted
line before its ^C, after a short prompt so the scrollback stays compact.
Everything from the first row of PS1 down is erased,
including the right prompt, the ghost, the hint rows, and any menu rows, and the
line is drawn again after the expansion of PS1_TRANSIENT. When PS1_TRANSIENT is
unset or empty, the short prompt is "$ ", or "# " for root, the expansion of
the \\$ escape and a space. The line keeps its highlighting, and a multi-row
PS1 collapses to the short prompt. The other keys that end a line leave it as
drawn. The option is off by default.
.PP
Ghost text uses prefix matches from completion and history. Tab also offers
smart-case and subsequence matches. An empty command position includes
keywords, builtins, functions, aliases, koshkit utilities, and programs from
PATH. An exact prefix ranks before a subsequence match. A directory candidate
always ends in a slash, and so do
.B .
and
.BR .. .
The operand of
.B cd
or
.B pushd
completes only directories, and when it is neither absolute nor led by a dot
or a tilde, also the directories under each CDPATH entry. The ghost suggestion
can use directories from CDPATH.
.PP
An explicit Tab first replaces the token with the longest common prefix. A sole
candidate replaces the token and takes the space the setting below asks for.
With no space, the Tab ends there, and the next Tab lists the entries of a
completed directory. With a space, the interactive selector stays open on the
candidates for the following word, without a preview of its first row until a
typed byte starts the word, and it closes quietly when that word has no
candidates. Typing, Esc, Ctrl\-G, and Enter act as in any open menu. When
that prefix does not extend the token and more than one candidate remains, the
candidates are presented by the selector chosen by
.B \-\-tab\-selector
or completion.menu_style. Ctrl\-Space acts as Tab on a POSIX system.
The completion.add_space_after_completed_word setting chooses whether
an accepted
complete candidate takes one space after it:
.B off
never, the default,
.B on
always, even after a directory's slash, so the menu does not descend into it,
and
.B on\-excluding\-trailing\-slash
unless the candidate ends in a slash.
.PP
The interactive selector draws a menu of the candidates below the line. The
completion menu starts under the token being completed, and starts at the left
edge when its widest row, the description column included, does not fit to the
right of that column, when an item of its first row would be cut there, or
when its first row would wrap onto more rows there than at the left edge. A
path list shows every path whole, as the line would hold it. The
history
menu always starts at the left edge. The
down arrow moves to the next row, the up arrow moves back, and Tab accepts the
highlighted row. In the completion menu, Escape closes the menu with the current
edit, and Ctrl\-G restores the line that opened it. Enter closes the completion
menu and runs the line as it stands. The highlighted row is not accepted. A
first
row identifies the active source and shows the available keys beside it, reading
.B selecting completions
for the candidates of a token and
.B incremental history search
for a stable snapshot of the shared history file. Opening the search does not
replace or merge the session's private history. The terminal height bounds the
number of rows, and a count row identifies the part that is drawn. A first row
too
long for the width wraps onto further rows between its items, each starting at
the column of the menu rows.
.PP
A printable key is inserted into the token, and the menu is drawn again from the
candidates that still match. A command-name, path, or koshconf option-name list
keeps the best tier that Tab ranks: the candidates the token opens
exactly, then the ones it opens in smart case, where a token without an
uppercase letter matches either case, then the ones that hold the bytes of a
name-like token of at least two bytes in order. Any other completion list, such
as the reply of a completion spec, keeps every candidate the token opens,
comparing letters without case. In the history search, an entry matches when
the query opens it, when the query appears inside it whole, or when the bytes
of the query appear inside it in order. The three kinds are drawn in that
order, and letters are compared without case. Backspace erases the last
character of the token
and widens the list again. A search that matches nothing keeps the menu open on
a dimmed row reading
.BR "no matches, erase to widen the search" ,
and a backspace brings the list back. A word the menu already listed is
listed again without asking the completion a second time. A key that leaves the
listed candidates, such as a blank, a slash, or an erase below the token, asks
the completion again once the typing has paused for 24 milliseconds, in every
word and for a list of any length. The line changes at once, the menu keeps its
rows until the new list arrives, and each further key moves that moment. A key
whose search finds no row shows a
.B loading...
row instead. Tab, the arrow keys, and a key that accepts a row ask at once, so
they always act on the list the line has now. An accepted candidate that refers to a
directory opens a menu of the entries it holds, or closes the menu when it
holds none. The menu closes when any other
key is pressed. That key then acts on the line.
.PP
The external selector passes the candidates to the program specified by
.B KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND
and its output replaces the token. Raw mode is left while the selector program
runs, and the prompt is repainted afterwards. A selector program that stops
follows the same continue and kill rules as the Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E editor. A
selector that reports no match or is dismissed leaves the line alone and prints
nothing. A single
remaining candidate keeps the plain listing. A session without a controlling
terminal keeps it too.
.PP
An empty
.BR KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND ,
a name that resolves to no program, and a selector that exits with a status
other than 0, 1, or 130 each report a message above the prompt and keep the
plain listing.
.PP
The plain selector prints the candidates below the prompt as a static column
list. The list starts under the token being completed. If the widest candidate
or description does not fit to the right of that column, the list starts at
the left edge. A terminal without decoration support always receives the plain
listing.
.PP
Ctrl\-R searches the history through the same selector. The line is the search
query, and the entries it matches are offered by the same three kinds, with the
newest first inside each kind. The interactive selector draws them in the menu.
Each offered entry is drawn with the syntax colors the line itself receives. The
selected row is drawn plain under its reverse video. Typing narrows the list,
and an accepted row replaces the whole line. The external selector receives the
same entries, and the line it prints replaces the whole line. The plain selector
keeps the incremental search inside the editor and accepts the same keys as the
menu. The down arrow and Ctrl\-R step back to an older match, the up arrow
and Ctrl\-F step forward to a newer one, Enter or Tab accepts the match without
running it, and Escape or Ctrl\-G cancels the search and restores the preceding
line state. A query that matches nothing opens the menu on its no-match row, so
a backspace can widen the search, and the incremental search writes
.B no match
after the query. An empty history leaves the line alone. A row or match too
long for the width ends in an ellipsis.
.PP
Completion identifies the command segment at the cursor across quoted
separators, command substitutions, and here-document bodies. Directory prefixes
that use quotes, escapes, variables, or tildes retain their typed form. An
explicit Tab at command position offers executable files and directories.
Syntax highlighting distinguishes syntax, commands, flags, variables, paths,
globs, and invalid input.
.PP
When the caret is on a parenthesis, bracket, or brace, or just after one, that
bracket and its partner are drawn bold black on a bright background. A caret on
the dollar sign of
.B $(
or
.B ${
counts as a caret on its bracket. The doubled forms
.BR [[ ,
.BR ]] ,
.BR ((
and
.B ))
are marked whole. A bracket inside quotes, a comment, or a here-document body,
or one escaped with a backslash, never pairs. An unmatched bracket keeps its
usual color.
.PP
Each vi line begins in insert mode. Escape enters command mode. Command mode
supports motions, counted operators, named registers, change repeat, and visual
selection. The cursor is a bar in insert mode, a block in command mode, and an
underline in visual mode or while an operator awaits a motion.
.PP
In command mode, arrow keys move between buffer rows without recalling history.
Insert-mode arrow keys recall history. With an operator pending, Up or Down
operates on the current line and the adjacent line. The dk and dj commands
perform the corresponding linewise operations.
.PP
The subsections below list every key the editor binds. A key not listed for a
mode does nothing there unless its subsection says otherwise. An unrecognized
key is a control key or an escape sequence the editor treats as no key at all,
such as Ctrl\-G, Ctrl\-O, a bare Escape, or a function key. The Windows
console also reports the arrows, Ctrl\-Left, Ctrl\-Right, Home, End, Delete,
Ctrl\-Delete, and Shift\-Tab as legacy scan codes, which act the same. A key
that a terminal reports through the kitty keyboard protocol or xterm
modifyOtherKeys
acts as the key it represents, as described for the
.B editor.request_extended_key_reports
option above.
