.SS Terminal shell integration
An interactive shell whose standard output is a terminal writes semantic marks
that terminals read to find prompts, commands, exit statuses, and the working
directory. Every mark ends with BEL. Each prompt is preceded by the end of the
previous command as ESC ] 133 ; D ; status, the working directory as
ESC ] 7 ; file://host/path, and the prompt start as ESC ] 133 ; A.
The prompt ends with ESC ] 133 ; B. A submitted command is announced by
ESC ] 133 ; C after PS0 is printed. The first prompt carries no D mark.
The marks occupy no columns in the prompt width. Under
.BR editor.transient_prompt_after_submit ,
the short prompt drawn over a submitted line starts on the row the A mark
named and ends with its own ESC ] 133 ; B, so the terminal finds the command
after it. When TERM_PROGRAM is vscode,
the shell also writes ESC ] 633 ; P ; Cwd= and ESC ] 633 ; E ; command line for
VS Code. No mark is written when standard output is not a terminal, when TERM
is dumb, or when
.B KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION
is 0.
.SH JOB CONTROL
Every stage of an asynchronous pipeline belongs to one job. POSIX stages share
one process group. The final stage determines $!, the ordinary pipeline status,
and the primary job identity. With pipefail, the rightmost failing stage may
determine the status.
.PP
In the bash, bash-posix, and sh moods an asynchronous simple command or
assignment forks before it expands its words, its prefix assignments, and its
redirections, as Bash and dash do. An expansion error then fails the job, with
status 1 in the bash moods and 2 in the sh mood, while $! still identifies it,
and
a side effect of the expansion, such as $((i++)) or ${v:=word}, stays in the
job. The kosh mood expands the words in the shell before it starts the job.
In the bash moods the job also runs the inherited EXIT trap before it ends with
such an error, as Bash does.
.PP
An ampersand ends a whole and-or list in every mood. In
.B a && b || c &
the entire list runs in one background job, so its left side never delays the
shell, its variable and directory changes stay in the job, and the
.B jobs
builtin shows the whole list. A single simple command still forks directly.
.PP
A stopped stage is recorded even when it is not the final one. The
.B wait
builtin returns that stored status without waiting for the process again. The
.B fg
and
.B bg
builtins resume every stage of the job.
.PP
.B jobs
and the notification of a finished or stopped job print one line per job in
the columns Bash uses: the job number and its current or previous mark, the
process with
.BR \-l ,
a state field 27 columns wide, and the command. A running job ends with an
ampersand. A job that a signal ended shows the description of the signal, such
as Terminated or Killed, with (core dumped) before the command when the process
dumped core, and wait still reports 128 plus the signal number. A job that
exited shows Done, or Exit and its status; the bash-posix and sh moods show
Done and the status in parentheses. With
.BR \-l ,
a stopped job shows the description of the signal that stopped it.
.PP
A job reported by
.BR wait ,
.BR jobs ,
or a notification leaves the job table, and the shell remembers the
status of its process. A later
.B wait
with that process returns the status again. The shell remembers the 1024 most
recent statuses, and
.B wait
without an operand forgets all of them along with the jobs it waits for.
Outside the sh mood, a job that had already finished when such a
.B wait
began stays in the table, so a later
.B wait
with its process still returns its status, as in Bash. A job number is one
more than the
highest number in use.
.B wait
reports 127 and a diagnostic for a process that is not a child of the shell or
a job that does not exist, and with several operands the last operand decides
the status.
.PP
A job that
.B wait
selects by number or process remains in the job table after it finishes. Bash
keeps it there until the next input line, the end of a loop iteration, an eval
or sourced command, a new child process, or the
.B jobs
builtin. A second
.B wait
for it before that point finds it by number again. The sh mood keeps it until
the next child process or
.B jobs
builtin, as dash does, and remembers no status once it leaves, so a later
.B wait
for its process reports 127. The sh mood also reports no diagnostic for a
process that is not a child.
.PP
A subshell, a pipeline stage, an asynchronous command, and a coprocess cannot
wait for a job of their parent. They report 127 for its process or number and
see none of the remembered statuses. In the sh mood a job number that matches
no job reports 2 from
.B wait
and
.BR jobs ,
as dash does.
.B jobs
there still lists the parent jobs as they were when the child started. A
command substitution reports 127 for a parent job that is still running, but
keeps the remembered statuses and the status of a parent job that had
finished, as Bash does. The sh mood treats a command substitution as a
subshell, as dash does.
.PP
.B wait \-n
returns the status of the next job to finish among the named jobs and
processes, or among every job when no operand is given. A job that already
finished and was not yet waited is returned first. A named process whose status
is remembered is returned at once. Otherwise every named job or process that
is not known is reported before the wait, and the wait goes on for the known
ones, so a named process substitution that finishes before a named job is
returned first. It reports 127 when no named or tracked job is left.
.B wait \-p
.I var
stores the process whose status is returned in
.IR var ,
and unsets it when no status is returned. With
.B \-n
and operands, a
.I var
that is a name reference loses the attribute first and holds the process
itself, as in Bash. A stopped job returns 128 plus the
stop signal, and
.B wait \-n
skips it.
.B wait \-f
waits until each job terminates. Without job control Bash waits for
termination even without
.BR \-f ,
so it never returns for a job that stays stopped.
.PP
A coprocess is one asynchronous job and sets $!. Its descriptors are numbered
above 9 and are closed across an exec. A subshell receives neither descriptor,
while NAME and NAME_PID stay readable there. An exec that closes one of them
sets its element of NAME to \-1. Once the coprocess has finished, the next wait,
jobs, new line, loop iteration, external command, subshell, pipeline,
asynchronous command, command substitution, or coprocess closes its remaining
descriptors and unsets NAME and NAME_PID, as Bash does when it reaps it.
.SH SIGNALS
The main shell ignores SIGPIPE. A forked child restores the default SIGPIPE
action. A producer terminated by SIGPIPE therefore returns status 141.
.PP
On Windows, a background process runs in its own console process group.
.PP
The CHLD trap runs its action once for every child the shell reaps, at the next
command boundary. Under
.B set -m
a job that stops counts as one arrival of its own, and a job that is continued
counts as none. No child signal is raised on Windows, and the reap alone
reaches that boundary. The Windows
.B kill
builtin sends only KILL and TERM. A script there cannot raise CHLD by hand. A
CHLD action that returns from the function it fired inside leaves the condition
installed, and the action fires again for the next child the shell reaps.
.PP
A trapped signal that arrives while
.B wait
blocks ends the wait. The action runs at the boundary the builtin returns to,
and
.B wait
reports 128 plus the signal number. The job it was waiting on is left running,
and the operands it has not reached are abandoned. A child arrival leaves the
wait blocked.
.PP
A redirection whose open blocks, such as a named pipe with no peer, resumes
after a trapped signal runs its action. The file is still opened and the
command runs against it. An untrapped interrupt ends the open, and the shell
reports the interrupt against the redirection.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors, warnings, and analysis diagnostics include source locations when a
mapping exists. A diagnostic may include a source line, caret, detail location,
and trailing note. A quoted source line is syntax colored and clipped to the
terminal width.
.PP
The analysis stage follows readable static source operands recursively. Dynamic
and unavailable operands are skipped.
.PP
An analysis diagnostic may include a ShellCheck identifier. A native analysis
diagnostic carries no identifier.
.PP
In the default mood, strict and lenient findings reject the script. Annoying
findings remain warnings. The annoying tier contains additional safety and style
checks that can produce many reports in existing scripts.
.PP
Whole-script dataflow findings are strict. In the default mood, a script is
rejected when it expands a name that it never assigns, calls a function that
uses
arguments without passing any, or calls a function before defining it. If an
unassigned name resembles an assigned name, that assignment is shown as a detail
location.
.PP
A function assignment without
.B local
is reported because it changes a global variable. Under a
.B sh
shebang or in the
.B sh
mood there is no
.BR local ,
so only a variable that is first set inside a function and also used outside it
is reported, with a suggestion to assign it before the function.
.PP
A comment of the form
.B # shellcheck disable=SC2086,unquoted-command-substitution
suppresses the named analysis checks. A numeric code suppresses every variant
under that code. A slug suppresses only its named variant. Native analysis
checks use their slugs. The optional
.B SC
prefix may be omitted. The value may be quoted, repeated, set to
.BR all ,
or contain a half-open range such as
.BR SC2000-SC2100 .
A directive before the first command applies to the complete file. A later
directive applies to the next complete and-or command. A directive after
.BR && ,
.BR || ,
or a pipe does not apply. These comments do not suppress parser or runtime
diagnostics outside the analysis catalog.
.PP
A diagnostic identifies the file, line, and column of the failing command. It
quotes its source line and puts a caret under it. Text inside command
substitution,
backticks, process substitution, and here-document bodies is part of the file,
so its diagnostics use the file's own line and column. Text built at run time,
such as an eval string or a trap action, is its own source. A location in an
eval string appears as
.BR eval:2:7: ,
counted from the start of that string, and the quoted line and caret come from
it. A location in a trap action appears as
.BR trap:1:5:
in the same way. A function defined by an eval keeps that numbering wherever it
is called, including from a background job, while
.B LINENO
and
.B BASH_LINENO
inside it count from the file line of the eval command, as Bash does. A
.B \-c
string is named
.BR \-c ,
so a location in it appears as
.BR \-c:1:5: .
Every
.B \-c
string uses that name, and the caret under the command line frame tells them
apart. Standard input and interactive lines have no name.
.PP
Every level of indirection between the top level and the failing command adds
one backtrace frame at the construct that entered it. The levels are a function
call, a sourced file, an eval, a trap action, a subshell, a command
substitution, a function substitution, and a process substitution. A trap
action also adds a frame at the
.B trap
command that installed it. A diagnostic with no indirection has no frame. A
script given on the command line adds no frame. The command line counts as a
level only when more than one script file or
.B \-c
string is given.
.PP
A frame begins with
.BR trace: .
Consecutive frames in one file print the file name once, in the first header of
the group, and the later headers show only the line and column. A repeated frame
is printed once and its header ends with the repeat count, as in
.BR "trace: repeated 4 times." .
A frame is printed once for each activation. Diagnostics print the same text
in every mood.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
On Windows an environment variable holds at most 32,766 UTF-16 units. The
shell keeps the whole value of a longer exported variable for itself, its
subshells, and its command substitutions, and a child process receives the
value cut to that limit at a character boundary.
.TP
.B KOSH_FLAGS
The value supplies default command-line options.
.TP
.B KOSH_ANALYSIS
The variable carries the analysis settings changed with
.B set
to the shells it launches. Each use of
.B set
rewrites it from the current state as whitespace-separated words:
.B mimicry
for
.BR \-I ,
.BI warnings= N
for the level of
.BR \-W ,
.B no\-annoying
for optimizer.show_annoying_tier off, and
.B no\-diagnostics
for optimizer.analyze_before_running off.
The variable is removed when no word remains. A shell loads it at startup, and
an explicit
.B \-W
level replaces the inherited level, and the other options add to the inherited
words. A shell that starts with the variable
rewrites it from its own settings.
.TP
.B KOSH_DEBUG_SYSTEM_KOSHCONF
A non-empty value selects the system configuration file in place of
/etc/kosh.conf or %ProgramData%\kosh\kosh.conf, with the same ownership check.
The test suite points it at a missing file. Only a debug build, made with
.B make MODE=dbg
or a Debug
.BR "zig build" ,
honors it; every other build, including a ReleaseSafe zig build, always uses
the system path.
.TP
.B KOSH_HISTORY_FILE
The value specifies the command history file. The default is ~/.kosh_history.
A value inherited from the environment wins over editor.history.file_path in the
settings files. Like bash's HISTFILE, the shell does not export it unless it
was inherited or is exported explicitly. HISTFILE itself has no effect on the
history file.
.TP
.B KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE
The value bounds the number of history events retained for recall, listing, and
storage. The default is 4096. A value of zero disables history retention and
storage. A value inherited from the environment wins over
editor.history.max_entries
in the settings files, and the shell exports it only when it was inherited or
is exported explicitly.
.TP
.B KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION
An interactive shell on a terminal marks each prompt, command, exit status, and
working directory with OSC 133 and OSC 7 sequences that terminals and IDE
terminals read. A value of 0 turns the marks off. They are never written when
standard output is not a terminal or TERM is dumb. See
.BR "Terminal shell integration" .
.TP
.BR FCEDIT ", " EDITOR
These variables select the editor used by
.BR fc .
FCEDIT takes precedence over EDITOR.
.TP
.B KOSH_DIRECTORY_HISTORY
The value specifies the z frecency store. The default is
~/.kosh_directory_history.
.TP
.B KOSH_CALC_HISTORY
The value specifies the interactive calc history file. The default is
~/.kosh_calc_history.
.TP
.BR KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND ", " KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_OPTS
These variables configure the external tab selector. An interactive session sets
both before the first prompt when a startup file has not already set them.
KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND specifies the program that receives the candidates
and defaults to
.BR fzf .
KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_OPTS holds the presentation options and defaults to
\-\-multi \-\-layout=reverse \-\-height=~40% \-\-min\-height=3. The value is
split on blanks with no expansion. The \-\-read0 and \-\-print0 record framing
is supplied separately and stays out of the value.
.TP
.BR ENV ", " BASH_ENV
These variables select startup files under the conditions in
.BR "STARTUP FILES" .
.TP
.B PATH
The value is a colon-separated command search path. An empty element, an
empty value, and an unset PATH search the current directory. The
.B \-\-no\-init\-files
option resets it to /usr/bin:/bin.
.TP
.BR HOME ", " PWD ", " OLDPWD ", " CDPATH
HOME supplies home paths. PWD and OLDPWD record the logical current and prior
directories. CDPATH supplies search directories for a relative
.B cd
operand. A relative directory is resolved against logical PWD. The form
.B cd ..
removes the last logical component, unless
.B cd \-P
or the physical option resolves symbolic links first;
.B cd \-L
keeps the logical form. A logical PWD is accepted only when it is
absolute and identifies the physical current directory.
.TP
.BR SHELL ", " SHLVL
SHELL specifies the executable. The invocation path is used when SHELL was
unset.
SHLVL is inherited and incremented by a child shell.
.TP
.B IFS
The value controls field splitting and the join performed by "$*". The default
contains space, tab, and newline.
.TP
.BR COLUMNS ", " LINES
An interactive shell stores the initial terminal width and height in these
variables.
.TP
.B PROMPT_COMMAND
The command runs before each interactive prompt. When the variable is an
indexed array, each nonempty element runs as a command in index order. Every
element sees the status and $_ of the previous command, and neither the status
nor $_ of a hook replaces them.
.TP
.BR PS0 ", " PS1 ", " PS2 ", " PS3 ", " PS4
These variables provide the pre-execution, primary, continuation, select, and
xtrace prompts. PS0 defaults to empty. PS2 defaults to "> ". PS3 defaults to
"#? ". PS4 defaults to "+ ". The first byte of PS4 is repeated once per
enclosing subshell. The default PS1 shows the git branch, ahead and behind
counts, user, host, and working directory, and it ends with a bullet. The
backslash escapes \\u,
\\h, \\H, \\w, \\W, \\P, \\g, \\$, \\?, \\j, \\L, \\., \\t, \\T, \\@, \\A,
\\d, \\s, \\v, \\V, \\!, \\#, \\l, \\n, \\r, \\e, \\a, \\[, and \\] are expanded.
The \\s escape inserts the last component of the path the shell was started
with, without a leading login dash or an .exe suffix, so it is the shell and
never the script in $0. The \\v escape inserts the major and minor version and
\\V adds the patch. In a Bash identity they come from BASH_VERSINFO, as in Bash,
and otherwise from the Koshka version, which gives 0.3 and 0.3.1/dev. The \\!
escape inserts the history number the next command receives, one more than the
newest event of the history, and 1 in a shell that keeps no history, such as a
script. The \\# escape inserts the command number. It starts at 1, and each
command line that the shell takes from a terminal, a script file, or standard
input adds one before it runs, where a line is a complete list ended by a
newline, so a; b counts once. An empty line, PROMPT_COMMAND, a trap, eval, a
sourced file, and a command in a subshell do not add one, and a string given
to
.B \-c
keeps 1. The \\l escape inserts the last component of the terminal device of
standard input, such as 3 for /dev/pts/3 or tty1, and tty when standard input
is not a terminal.
The \\D{format} escape passes format to strftime and inserts the result, as
Bash does. An empty format gives the time in the locale, and a format with no
closing brace extends to the end of the prompt. A \\D that no brace follows
stays as typed. The \\L escape inserts the duration of the last command, which
is empty for a command that took under 5 milliseconds.
The escapes are decoded before parameters and substitutions expand, so text an
escape inserts is never expanded, and a backslash that a parameter or
substitution yields is never decoded.
A raw 001 or 002 byte in PS0, PS1, RPS1, or PS1_TRANSIENT bounds an invisible
region the way \\[ and \\] do: it is not drawn, and the escape sequences
inside the region take no columns.
The \\. escape emits a bullet, plain when the last exit status is zero and
bold red when it is not. The user, host, directory, branch, shell name, and
version that \\u, \\h, \\H, \\w, \\W, \\P, \\g, \\s, \\v, and \\V insert draw a
C0 control byte or DEL in caret notation, such as ^[ for escape, and a C1
control
or a byte that is not UTF-8 as \\xHH, so a directory name cannot write escape
sequences to the terminal.
.TP
.BR RPS1 ", " RPROMPT
RPS1 is the right prompt drawn at the right edge of the first input row.
RPROMPT is read when RPS1 is unset or empty. The value expands like PS1. See
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B PS1_TRANSIENT
The value is the prompt a submitted line is redrawn after when the
.B editor.transient_prompt_after_submit
option is on. It expands like PS1. When it is unset or empty, the prompt is
"$ ", or "# " for root. See
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B BASH_XTRACEFD
The value selects the xtrace descriptor. Standard error is used when it is unset
or invalid.
.TP
.BR TERM ", " NO_COLOR
TERM selects terminal capabilities on POSIX systems. A nonempty NO_COLOR
disables prompt, diagnostic, koshkit cat, and editor color. Reverse video
carries no color. The reversed row of the interactive selector stays visible.
.TP
.B MANPATH
Completion searches these manual paths for subcommands and options.
.TP
.B OPTERR
A value of zero silences the diagnostics of
.BR getopts .
A leading colon in the option string silences them too.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
The value specifies the directory used for a temporary file. The default is
/tmp.
.TP
.BR PIPESTATUS ", " REPLY ", " OPTIND ", " OPTARG ", " TIMEFORMAT
These variables hold pipeline statuses, default read and select input, getopts
state, and time formatting.
.TP
.BR KOSH ", " KOSH_VERSION ", " KOSH_COMMIT ", " KOSH_BUILD_MODE ", " KOSH_OS
These values identify the invocation path, version, build commit, build mode,
and build host distribution and kernel. The invocation path is absolute. A name
without a slash is found on PATH, and the running executable is used when the
search finds nothing. The shell never exports them: a value inherited from the
environment is replaced and loses its export attribute, so a child shell that
is not Koshka sees them unset.
.TP
.B KOSH_GIT_BRANCH
For a working directory inside a repository, the value contains the branch
name. A detached HEAD produces a short commit hash. The value is empty outside a
repository. An assignment overrides detection. The search for a repository
moves up from the working directory and never enters a directory listed in
GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES. That variable holds absolute paths separated by colons,
the same format as PATH.
.TP
.BR KOSH_GIT_AHEAD ", " KOSH_GIT_BEHIND
The values contain the unpushed and unpulled commit counts relative to the
current branch's upstream. Both are empty outside a repository, when no upstream
is configured, and when the count is zero.
.TP
.B KOSH_IDENTITY
The read-only exported value is the lowercase CRC-32 identity of the running
executable. An inherited value is discarded.
.TP
.B KOSHCONF
Each read supplies the mood and the interactive options in the base64 binary
form that
.BR kosh (5)
describes. An assignment is discarded, and the shell never exports it by
itself. A KOSHCONF value in the environment at startup is applied after the
configuration files and removed from the environment, so
.B ssh host KOSHCONF=\(dq$KOSHCONF\(dq kosh
carries the settings to a remote shell.
.B koshconf load
applies a value to the current session.
.TP
.BR KOSH_WELCOME ", " KOSH_FAREWELL
When set and non-empty, the value is printed once at interactive startup
(KOSH_WELCOME) or exit (KOSH_FAREWELL), prefixed with the shell name. An empty
value prints nothing. When unset, the shell prints the built-in greeting for
the active mood on startup and a fixed farewell on exit. A non-zero exit code
is appended in parentheses to the farewell.
.TP
.B KOSH_ANSI_*
The names BLACK, RED, GREEN, YELLOW, BLUE, MAGENTA, CYAN, WHITE, their BRIGHT_
forms, BOLD, DIM, and RESET supply terminal escapes. A recognized name is empty
when color is disabled. An explicit assignment takes precedence.
.TP
.BR RANDOM ", " SRANDOM ", " SECONDS ", " EPOCHSECONDS ", " EPOCHREALTIME
These dynamic values supply random numbers, elapsed time, and wall clock time.
An assignment to
.B SECONDS
moves the base the elapsed count is measured from. An assignment to
.B RANDOM
seeds the generator, and a repeated seed repeats the sequence. Each subshell
and command substitution reseeds its own generator, so its numbers differ from
the ones the parent draws next. A
.B local
declaration of either name freezes it for the length of the call. An
.B unset
of
.B SECONDS
or
.B RANDOM
makes the name ordinary for the rest of the shell. The name is then empty,
an assignment stores an ordinary value, and a subshell inherits it that way.
They are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR IFS ", " LINENO
The field separator and the line number of the running command are available in
every mood. Inside a command substitution, backticks, or a process
substitution,
.B LINENO
is the file line of the command that expands it in every mood. Bash differs
inside a command substitution that spans lines. It starts the count at a line
between the first and last line of the substitution, which its parser chooses,
and counts the lines of the body as it prints the body back, without comments
or blank lines. A command on the second line of
.B y=$(echo $LINENO
followed by
.B echo $LINENO)
gives 3 in Bash and 2 in kosh when the substitution starts on line 1.
.TP
.BR BASHPID ", " PPID ", " UID ", " EUID ", " GROUPS ", " HOSTNAME ", " HOSTTYPE ", " MACHTYPE ", " OSTYPE
These dynamic values identify the current shell process, parent, ids,
groups, host, and platform. The value of $$ remains the original shell process
id. A subshell that is the last command of an already forked substitution,
subshell, pipeline stage, or background job runs in that process without
another fork, so
.B BASHPID
inside it repeats the value of the enclosing process. These names are
unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B GLOBIGNORE
The value lists colon\-separated patterns that pathname expansion removes from
its results. Assignment enables
.B dotglob
and unsetting disables it. The pathname expansion section describes the
matching.
.TP
.B HISTCMD
The value is the history number of the current command. A non-interactive shell
reports zero. An assignment is discarded. The name is unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR SHELLOPTS ", " BASHOPTS ", " BASH_SUBSHELL ", " BASH_ARGV0 ", " BASH_EXECUTION_STRING ", " BASH_MONOSECONDS
These values expose set options, shopt options, subshell depth, argument zero,
the current command string, and a monotonic millisecond count. The two option
lists are read-only. These names are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR COPROC ", " COPROC_PID
The
.B coproc
command binds these to the descriptors and the process id of an unnamed
coprocess. A named coprocess binds NAME and NAME_PID and leaves these unset.
These names are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B BASH_ALIASES
This associative array contains the current aliases. Assigning an element
defines or replaces that alias. Unsetting an element has no effect. Unsetting
the whole variable disconnects the array without deleting the aliases. A later
.B declare \-A BASH_ALIASES
creates an ordinary array that is not connected to aliases. The name is
unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR BASH_ARGC ", " BASH_ARGV
.B BASH_ARGC
contains the positional parameter count for each recorded call frame.
.B BASH_ARGV
contains the recorded arguments in reverse order, with the innermost frame
first. The root shell is the last frame. Changing positional parameters does
not change arguments already recorded for a frame.
.PP
The
.B extdebug
shopt option records function calls and sourced-file calls. A source call with
operands records those operands. A source call without operands records its
path as one argument. Without
.BR extdebug ,
function calls are not recorded. A source call without operands records its
path while the file runs. The first root source call with operands establishes
those operands as the base frame. Later source calls with operands are not
recorded. Enabling
.B extdebug
before the arrays have been initialized starts with the current frame.
.PP
Assignments and declarations with values have no effect. Unset and local
declarations fail. The names are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B DIRSTACK
This indexed array contains the current directory at index zero followed by the
saved directories that
.B dirs
prints. Assigning index zero or an index past the end has no effect. Assigning
another existing index changes that saved stack entry. Whole-array assignment
changes only the existing indices, and append cannot grow the stack. Unsetting
an element has no effect. A local declaration shadows the live array until the
function returns. Unsetting the whole variable disconnects the array without
clearing the directory stack. A later
.B declare \-a DIRSTACK
creates an ordinary array that is not connected to the stack. The name is
unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR FUNCNAME ", " BASH_SOURCE ", " BASH_LINENO ", " BASH_COMMAND
These values describe the active call stack and command. Their array forms
contain the complete stack. The text of BASH_COMMAND is the source as it was
written. A compound command is laid out by Bash over several indented lines,
and that layout is not reproduced. They are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B BASH_REMATCH
The array contains groups from the last [[ ... =~ ... ]] match.
.TP
.BR BASH ", " BASH_VERSION ", " BASH_VERSINFO
These values report a Bash identity. They are initialized when the runtime mood
or an initialized mood is
.B bash
or
.BR bash-posix .
.TP
.BR COMP_WORDS ", " COMP_CWORD ", " COMP_LINE ", " COMP_POINT ", " COMP_WORDBREAKS ", " COMPREPLY
A programmable completion function uses these values and writes COMPREPLY.
COMP_WORDS contains the words on the line. COMP_CWORD is the index of the word
under the cursor. COMP_LINE contains the complete line. COMP_POINT is the
cursor byte offset. COMP_WORDBREAKS contains the bytes that split a word.
Trailing spaces are removed from each COMPREPLY entry, and the space after an
accepted candidate is controlled by the
completion.add_space_after_completed_word option and the nospace option
of
the completion. The
spec's
.B \-X
filter and its
.B \-P
and
.B \-S
text apply to COMPREPLY entries as they do to the spec's other candidates. When
the spec was registered with
.B complete -o filenames
or
.B compopt -o filenames
is run inside the function, a slash is appended to each entry that refers to a
directory, and to each such word of a
.B \-W
list.
.B compopt +o filenames
turns the slash off for the current completion, also for the candidates of a
directory or file action and a glob. The directories that dirnames and
plusdirs add after the function keep their slash, and a
.B compopt
run inside the function turns dirnames, plusdirs, default, and bashdefault on
or off for them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The shell exits with the status of the last command it ran. A command path that
refers to a directory, or has a trailing separator whose target is not a
directory,
returns 126. A script file that cannot be opened returns 127. A syntax error in
a script or a
.B \-c
string returns 2, and so does an
.B eval
or a sourced file whose text does not parse. With the posix option on, such
an
.B eval
or
.B .
also ends a non-interactive shell with status 2, as Bash in POSIX mode and
dash do, unless
.B command
runs it. A usage error in a
non-rescue invocation returns 2. In the bash and bash-posix moods, an
expansion error that ends the shell, such as an unset parameter under nounset,
a
.B ${name:?}
operand, or a bad substitution, returns 127 from a
.B \-c
string, as Bash does, and 1 from a script file or standard input. Errexit keeps
the status 1 for a
.B \-c
string. A background simple command or assignment of a
.B \-c
string that meets such an error also ends with 127, while a background
subshell or group ends with 1. A child terminated by SIGPIPE returns 141. A
.B \-\-lint
batch returns 1 when an input cannot be read or parsed, or its analysis reports
an error. Warning-only findings return 0.
The
.B \-\-format
mode returns 1 when an input cannot be read, parsed, formatted, or replaced.
The
.B \-\-apply
mode also returns 1 when a safe replacement cannot be completed. These modes
return 0 after every requested input succeeds. An invalid option combination
returns 2.
.SH ERRORS
An unavailable command or pathname is reported through its first unavailable
component when that component can be identified. A missing explicit directory
may include one close sibling suggestion. A source caret covers the relevant
component or argument when source mapping is available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_history
This is the default command history file.
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_directory_history
This is the default z frecency store.
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_calc_history
This is the default interactive calc history file.
.TP
.IR /etc/kosh.conf ", " $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf ", " ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf
These are the system and user settings files. Windows loads the system file
from %ProgramData%\kosh\kosh.conf.
.PP
The settings files and the shell startup files are described in
.BR kosh (5).
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The
.B kosh
and
.B bash
moods accept Bash syntax in addition to POSIX syntax. The
.B bash-posix
mood uses Bash syntax with the POSIX option enabled. The
.B sh
mood uses the supported dash-compatible behavior.
.PP
The 0b arithmetic prefix is an implementation extension. Bash writes the same
value with a 2# prefix. The
.BR enable ,
.BR bind ,
and selected
.B help
flags are accepted compatibility forms with the effects described under
.BR BUILTINS .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kosh (5),
.BR bash (1),
.BR dash (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR fish (1),
.BR busybox (1)
